hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4201","Narrated al-Muqaddam ibn Dawud, from Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Ibn Lahiah, from Musa ibn Jubayr, that Abd Allah ibn Ayyash told him, from Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Muawiyah, from Dihyah al-Kalbi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Qabati garments and gave me a Qubtiyyah. He said: Split it into two splits; cut one of them into a shirt and give the other to your wife to veil herself with. When I turned he said: Command your wife to place beneath her split a garment that does not reveal her."
"4202","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani, from Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi, from Yahya ibn al-Durays, from Anbasah ibn Said, from Jabir, from Amir, from Dihyah al-Kalbi, who said: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a woolen cloak and two leather socks; he wore them until they wore out, and did not ask about them whether we had purified them or not."
"4203","Daghfal ibn Hanzalah. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, from Shayban ibn Farrukh, from Abu Hilal al-Rasibi, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, that Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to Daghfal and asked him about Arabic and the lineages of people, and asked him about the stars — and he was learned. He said: O Daghfal, whence did you preserve this? He said: I preserved it with an asking tongue and an understanding heart; the bane of knowledge is forgetfulness. He said: Go with Yazid and teach him Arabic, the lineages of Quraysh, and the stars."
"4204","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, from Ali ibn al-Madini, (ha) and narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shuayb al-Simsar, both saying: narrated Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri, all saying: narrated Muadh ibn Hisham, from my father, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Daghfal ibn Hanzalah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-five years."
"4205","Narrated Musa ibn Harun and Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, both saying: narrated Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, from Abu Hisham al-Rifai, both saying: narrated Muadh ibn Hisham, from my father, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Daghfal ibn Hanzalah, who said: The Christians had the fast of the month of Ramadan incumbent upon them. A king ruled them and fell ill, and said: If Allah heals him, he shall add ten. Then a king ruled them after him, ate meat and ailed, and said: If Allah heals him, he shall add eight days. Then a king ruled after him and said: Why should we leave any of these days but complete them and make our fast in spring? So he did, and they became fifty days."
"4206","Daylam ibn Fayruz al-Himyari. Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Misri, from Abu Asim, (ha) and narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, both saying: narrated Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, from Marthad ibn Abdillah al-Yazani, that Daylam al-Himyari told them that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, we are in a cold land, and we drink a drink by which we gain strength. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Does it intoxicate? He said: Yes. He said: Then do not approach it. Then he repeated the question to him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does it intoxicate? He said: Yes. He said: Do not approach it. He said: But they will not be patient without it. He said: Whoever is not patient without it, then kill him."
"4207","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, (ha) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, both saying: narrated Muhammad ibn Ubayd, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Hannad ibn al-Sari and Abu Kurayb, both saying: narrated Abda ibn Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdillah al-Yazani, from Daylam al-Himyari, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, a drink from this wheat by which we gain strength for our work and against the cold of our land. He said: Does it intoxicate? I said: Yes. He said: Then avoid it. Then I came to him from in front of him and said the like of that to him. He said: Does it intoxicate? I said: Yes. He said: Then avoid it. I said: The people will not abandon it. He said: If they do not abandon it, then kill them."
"4208","Narrated Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi, from Qutayba ibn Sa'id, from Ibn Lahi'a, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib and Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Abu al-Khayr, from Daylam al-Jayshani, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we are in a cold land of severe cold; we make therein a drink from wheat - is drinking it lawful? He said: Does it intoxicate you? I said: Yes indeed. He said: Then it is khamr (wine)."
"4209","Dukayn ibn Sa'id al-Muzani. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa, from al-Humaydi, from Sufyan, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard Qays saying: Dukayn ibn Sa'id told me, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with four hundred riders asking him for food. He said: O Umar, go and feed them and give them. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have with me only some sa's of dates which would not suffice my family. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Listen and obey. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Hearing and obeying. So Umar went until he came to an upper room, and took out a key from his waist-wrapper and opened it, and said to the people: Enter. They entered, and I was the last of the people to enter; I held back, then I turned and there was like a young camel's calf (a heap) of dates."
"4210","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, from Abdillah ibn Abi Numayr, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Dukayn ibn Sa'id al-Muzani, who said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him for food, and we were four hundred riders; then he mentioned the like of it."
"4211","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqāl al-Harrani, from Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, from Isa ibn Yunus, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Dukayn ibn Sa'id al-Muzani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4212","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Dukayn ibn Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4213","Dirham Abu Mu'awiya. Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, from Sulayman ibn Harb, from Muhammad ibn Talha, from Mu'awiya ibn Dirham, that Dirham came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have come to you to seek your ruling about going out to battle. He said: Do you have a mother? He said: Yes. He said: Then stay with her."
"4214","The chapter of the letter Dhal: those whose name is Dhu'ayb. Dhu'ayb ibn Qabisa al-Khuza'i, Abu Qabisa ibn Dhu'ayb the jurist. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Sinan ibn Salama, from Ibn Abbas, from Dhu'ayb al-Khuza'i, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent sacrificial camels with him and said: If any of them breaks down and you fear its death, then slaughter it, then dip its sandal in its blood, then strike its side with it, and do not eat of it yourself nor anyone of your company, and distribute it."
"4215","Narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Muhammad ibn al-Minhal, from Yazid ibn Zuray', (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami' al-Misri, from Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Sadusi, from Khalid ibn al-Harith, (ha) and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, both saying: from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, (ha) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, both saying: from Muhammad ibn Bishr, all saying: from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Sinan ibn Salama, from Ibn Abbas, that Dhu'ayb al-Khuza'i told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send sacrificial camels with him, then he would say: If any of them breaks down and you fear death, then slaughter it, then dip its sandal in its blood, then strike its side with it, and do not eat of it yourself nor anyone of your company, and distribute it. The wording is that of the hadith of Yazid ibn Zuray'; and Khalid ibn al-Harith said: Dhu'ayb ibn Qabisa."
"4216","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Abu Kurayb, from Mu'awiya ibn Hisham, from Hamza al-Zayyat, from Abu Ishaq: Dhu'ayb told me that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was near death, Safiyya said: O Messenger of Allah, every woman among your wives has a family to whom she can turn, and you have removed my family; so if an event occurs, to whom (shall I turn)? He said: To Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4217","Dhu'ayb al-Anbari. Narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Ata ibn Khalid ibn al-Zubayr ibn Abdillah ibn Rudayh ibn Dhu'ayb al-Anbari, at Basra: my father Khalid told me, from his father al-Zubayr, from his father Abdillah, from his father Rudayh, from his father Dhu'ayb, that the delegation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Umm Zubayb and took her carpet; so Zubayb caught up with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, the delegation took my mother's carpet. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return to him his mother's carpet. Then he (Zubayb) took from the one who had taken his mother's carpet a sa' of barley, and his sword and his belt. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hand and wiped the head of Zubayb with it, then said: May Allah bless you, O boy, and bless your mother. Musa ibn Harun said: al-Zarbiyya is a furnishing heavier than al-Zaylawiyya; Allah the Exalted said: {and rich carpets spread out}, meaning spread."
"4218","Narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Ata ibn Khalid: my father Khalid told me, from his father al-Zubayr, from his father Abdillah, from his father Rudayh, from his father Dhu'ayb, that A'isha said: O Prophet of Allah, I want a freed slave of the children of Isma'il specifically. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Wait until the fay' (spoil) of al-Anbar comes tomorrow. Then the fay' of al-Anbar came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Take from them four handsome bright youths whose heads are not to be separated. Ata ibn Khalid said: So she took my grandfather Rudayh, and she took my cousin Samura, and she took my cousin Rukhayy, and she took my maternal uncle Zubayb. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hand and wiped their heads with it and blessed them, then said: These, O A'isha, are of the children of Isma'il specifically."
"4219","Dhakwan, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); there is disagreement about his name: it is said Mihran and it is said Tahman. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Minjab ibn al-Harith, from Sharik, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: Something was bequeathed to me for Banu Hashim, so I came to Abu Ja'far at Madina, and he sent me to a woman of them, a daughter of Ali, an aged old woman. He said: The freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called Tahman or Dhakwan, told me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for me nor for the people of my household, and the freed slave of a people is of themselves."
"4220","Dhakwan ibn Abd Qays al-Ansari, a Badri; he was martyred on the day of Uhud. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi, from Muhammad ibn Fulayh, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqaba, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn Abd Qays ibn Khalda."
"4221","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, from his father, from Ibn Lahi'a, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqaba, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn Abd Qays ibn Khalda; and he had gone out from Madina to Makka emigrating to Allah, and he had attended Badr."
"4222","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, from his father, from Ibn Lahi'a, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who were martyred with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn Abd Qays."
"4223","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi, from Muhammad ibn Fulayh, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who were martyred on the day of Uhud, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn Abd Qays."
"4224","Dhar' Abu Talha al-Khawlani; there is disagreement about his companionship. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Warraq, from Abu Umar al-Darir, from Hammad ibn Salama, from Abu Sinan Isa, from Abu Talha al-Khawlani, whose name is Dhar', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be four armies, so عليكم بالشام (hold to al-Sham), for Allah the Exalted has guaranteed al-Sham for me."
"4225","Dhu al-Yadayn; it is said his name is al-Khirbaq, and his kunya is Abu al-Uryan. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla: Abu Shayba told us, from al-Hakam, who said: Ammar said: We were three with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), all of us adbat (ambidextrous). It was said to Abu Shayba: What is al-adbat? He said: The one who works with both his hands: Dhu al-Shimalayn, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Abu Layla."
"4226","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Mu'di ibn Sulayman, from Shu'ayb ibn Mutayr, from his father Mutayr, and Mutayr being present confirming him in his statement, who said: How did you tell me? He said: O my father, you told me that Dhu al-Yadayn met you at Dhi Khashab, and told you that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them one of the two afternoon prayers, and it was Asr, and prayed two rak'as, then gave salutation, and the hasty people went out saying: The prayer has been shortened. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah have mercy on them) followed him. Then Dhu al-Yadayn caught up with him and said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened or did you forget? He said: The prayer has not been shortened, nor did I forget. Then he turned to Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) and said: What does Dhu al-Yadayn say? They said: He has spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, and the people came back, and he prayed two rak'as, then gave salutation, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"4227","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, from Yazid ibn Zuray', from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salutation after three rak'as of Asr, then entered. A man called al-Khirbaq, who was long-handed, said to him: Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah? So he came out angry, dragging his cloak, and said: Is he truthful? They said: Yes. So he stood and prayed that rak'a, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4228","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Qatan ibn Nusayr, from Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad al-Qirdusi, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, who said: I was with al-Hasan when a man passed by him and spoke to him; he said: Dhu al-Yadayn said: O company of the Ansar, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not command you to be patient until you meet him?"
"4229","Dhu Mikhmar, and it is said: Mikhbar, son of the brother of al-Najashi. Narrated Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti, from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi', from Hariz ibn Uthman, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Abu Hayy al-Mu'adhdhin, from Dhi Mikhbar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This authority was in Himyar, then Allah removed it from them and placed it in Quraysh."
"4230","Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki, from Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi, from Maslama ibn Alqama al-Mazini, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman, the freed slave of Banu Hashim: Dhu Mikhmar, son of the brother of al-Najashi, told us, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a battle; they traveled by night as they traveled, then they alighted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: O Dha Mikhmar. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and at your aid. He took hold of the head of my she-camel and said: Sit here, and do not be a worthless guard tonight. So I took hold of the head of the she-camel, but my eyes overcame me, and I slept, and the she-camel slipped away and went. I did not wake except by the heat of the sun. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: O Dha Mikhmar. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and at your aid. He said: By Allah, tonight you were a worthless one, as I said. Then we moved away from that place, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us. When he finished the prayer, he supplicated that the she-camel be returned, and a gust of wind came with it driving it. When it was the next day, when dawn shone, he ordered Bilal, and he called the adhan, then he ordered him and he made the iqama, then he prayed with us. When he finished the prayer, he said: This is our prayer of yesterday. Then he began anew the prayer of that day."
"4231","Narrated Musa ibn Isa ibn al-Mundhir al-Himsi, from his father, from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, from Safwan ibn Amr, who said: Rashid ibn Sa'd told me: Dhu Mikhmar told me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will make a secure peace with the Romans until you and they raid an enemy together, and you will be given victory, and you will alight in a meadow with hills."
"4232","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, from Isa ibn Yunus, from al-Awza'i, from Hassan ibn Atiyya: Khalid ibn Ma'dan told us, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Dhi Mikhbar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: You will make a secure peace with the Romans, then you and they will raid an enemy, and you will be given victory and take spoils and be safe. Then you will turn back until you alight in a meadow with hills; then a man of the people of Christianity will raise a cross and say: The cross has overcome. Then a man of the Muslims will become angry and stand up to him and smash it. Thereupon the Romans will betray, and they will gather for the great battle."
"4233","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda, from his father, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani, from Dhi Mikhbar, son of the brother of al-Najashi, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: You will make peace with the Romans for ten years, a secure peace; they will fulfill for two years and betray in the third, or they will fulfill for four and betray in the fifth. An army of you will alight in their city, and you and they will raid an enemy behind you and behind them, and you will fight that enemy, and Allah will grant you victory. Then you will turn back with what you have obtained of reward and spoil, and you will alight in a meadow with hills. Your speaker will say: Allah has overcome, and their speaker will say: The cross has overcome. They will contend over it repeatedly. The Muslims will become angry, with their cross not far from them; that Muslim will rush to their cross and smash it, and they will come forth against the breaker of their cross and strike his neck. That band of Muslims will rush to their weapons, and the Romans will rush to their weapons, and they will kill that band of Muslims as martyrs. Then they will come to their king and say: We have sufficed you against the might of the Arabs and their strength; so what are we waiting for? Then he will gather for you the carrying of a woman (a huge host), then they will come to you under eighty banners, under every banner twelve thousand."
"4234","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti, from his father, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Isma'il ibn Rafi', from Ibn Muhayriz, from Dhi Mikhbar, son of the brother of al-Najashi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4235","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb, (ha) and narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi, from his father, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, both saying: from Hariz ibn Uthman, from Yazid ibn Sulayh, from Dhi Mikhbar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4236","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Massisi, from Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi, from Hashim ibn Amr al-Himsi: Isma'il ibn Ayyash told us: Sa'id ibn Sinan told me, from Abu al-Zahiriyya, from Dhi Mikhbar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah the Exalted looked upon the people of Madina when it was a plain before it was built, with in it neither mud-brick nor wool (no buildings nor flocks), and said: O people of Yathrib, I stipulate upon you three things, and I drive to you of all fruits: do not disobey, and do not hoard in greed, and do not be arrogant. If you do any of that, I will leave you like a slaughtered camel, none refraining from eating it."
"4237","Dhu al-Lihya al-Kilabi ibn Amr ibn Qurt ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abdillah ibn Kilab. Narrated Muhammad ibn Hammuya Abu Sayyar al-Tustari, from Shabab al-Usfuri, from Sahl ibn Aslam al-Adawi, from Yazid ibn Abi Mansur, from Dhi al-Lihya al-Kilabi, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do we act in a matter newly begun, or in a matter already concluded? He said: Rather in a matter already concluded. He said: Then for what is action? He said: Act, for everyone is facilitated to that for which he was created."
"4238","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Yahya ibn Ma'in, from Abu Ubayda al-Haddad, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim, from Yazid ibn Abi Mansur, from Dhi al-Lihya al-Kilabi, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, do we act in a matter newly begun, or in a matter already concluded? He said: No, rather in a matter already concluded. He said: Then for what is action? He said: Act, for everyone is facilitated to that for which he was created."
"4239","Dhu al-Asabi', and he is Dhu al-Zawa'id. Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb, from Uthman ibn Ata, that Ziyad ibn Abi Sawda told him, from Abu Imran, from Dhi al-Asabi', that he said: O Messenger of Allah, if we are tried by remaining after you, what do you command us? He said: عليكم ببيت المقدس (hold to Bayt al-Maqdis); perhaps Allah will grant you offspring who go to it in the morning and return in the evening."
"4240","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from al-Hakam ibn Musa, from Damra ibn Rabi'a, from Uthman ibn Ata, from Abu Imran, from Dhi al-Asabi', who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, if we are tried after you by remaining, where do you command us? He said: عليكم ببيت المقدس (hold to Bayt al-Maqdis); perhaps there will arise for you offspring who go to that mosque in the morning and return in the evening."
"4241","Narrated Abu Amir Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Nahwi al-Suri, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Sulaym ibn Mutayr of the people of Wadi al-Qura, from his father: I heard Dha al-Zawa'id saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage commanding the people and forbidding them, then he said: Have I conveyed? They said: O Allah, yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness. Then he said: Take the stipend as long as it is fresh; when Quraysh contend among themselves over kingship and the stipend becomes a bribe for your religion, then leave it."
"4242","The chapter of the letter Ra: those whose name is Rafi'. Rafi' ibn Khadij ibn Rafi' al-Ansari, kunya Abu Abdillah; of his reports. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi, from his father, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Uthman ibn Ubaydillah ibn Rafi', who said: I saw Rafi' ibn Khadij dyeing with yellow."
"4243","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib, from Muhammad ibn Talha ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdillah ibn Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he went out on the day of Uhud, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to send him back, deeming him too young. His uncle said to him: O Messenger of Allah, he is an archer. So he let him go out, and he was struck by an arrow in his chest or his throat. His uncle came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My brother's son has been struck by an arrow. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you leave it in him and he dies, he will die a martyr. Abdillah ibn Husayn said: And his wife told me that she used to see him washing, and it (the arrowhead) would move in his chest."
"4244","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar, from Abu al-Walid and Muhammad ibn Kathir, all saying: from Amr ibn Marzuq al-Washiji: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij told us, from his grandmother, the wife of Rafi', that Rafi' was shot with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, or the day of Khaybar - Amr doubted - with an arrow in his collarbone (thunduwa). He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, remove the arrow. He said: O Rafi', if you wish, I will remove the arrow and the arrowhead together, and if you wish, I will remove the arrow and leave the arrowhead, and I will bear witness for you on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) removed the arrow and left the arrowhead. He lived with it until the caliphate of Mu'awiya (may Allah be pleased with him), when the wound reopened and he died after Asr. Ibn Umar was approached and it was said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, Rafi' ibn Khadij has died. He invoked mercy upon him and said: One like Rafi' is not to be taken out until those around Madina of the villages are informed. When we went out with his funeral and prayed over him, Ibn Umar came until he sat at the head of the grave. A freed woman of ours screamed, and Ibn Umar said: What is wrong with the foolish woman? Let none harm the shaykh, for he has no endurance for the punishment of Allah."
"4245","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Ahmad ibn Sinan, from Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri, from Rifa'a ibn Hurayr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: My grandfather told us, from his father, who said: My uncle and I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was intending Badr. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I want to go out with you. He kept closing his hand and saying: I deem you too young, and I do not know what you will do when you meet the enemy? I said: Do you know that I am the best shot of those who shoot? So he sent me back."
"4246","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Abu Kamil al-Jahdari, from Khalid ibn Yazid al-Haddadi: Abu Amr told me that he attended the funeral of Rafi' ibn Khadij with women weeping. Abdillah ibn Umar stood and said: Rafi' ibn Khadij is an old shaykh with no endurance for the punishment of Allah."
"4247","Narrated Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj, from Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Rafi' ibn Khadij died in the year seventy-three, at Madina."
"4248","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqa, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari, from al-Waqidi, who said: And in it died Rafi' ibn Khadij at the beginning of this year, and Ibn Umar (may Allah have mercy on him) attended his funeral - meaning the year seventy-three - and Rafi' was eighty-six on the day he died."
"4249","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr, who said: Rafi' ibn Khadij died in the year seventy-four, at its beginning."
"4250","And what Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated; Abdillah ibn Umar from Rafi'. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Nu'aym, from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: We did not dislike sharecropping until I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sharecropping."
"4251","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, (ha) and narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisī, from Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq, both saying: from Sufyan ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, who said: We used to sharecrop (mukhabara) and saw no harm in it, until Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it, and we abandoned it because of his statement."
"4252","Narrated Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi, from Sulayman ibn Harb, (ha) and narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, both saying: from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: We saw no harm in khibr (sharecropping) until last year, when Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"4253","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from his father, (ha) and narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, both saying: from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: We saw no harm in khibr, until Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed last year that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"4254","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Khaythama, from Umar ibn Yahya al-Ubulli, from Abd al-Warith, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, who said: We saw no harm in khibr, then Rafi' ibn Khadij said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us."
"4255","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Khalil, from Mujahid, who said: Ibn Umar said: Rafi' ibn Khadij has indeed deprived us of the benefit of our land."
"4256","Narrated Abdillah ibn Najiya, from Ahmad ibn Mansur, from Abu Asim, from Sufyan, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"4257","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mukram, from Mahmud ibn Ghaylan, from al-Nadr ibn Shumayl: Ash'ath told us, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: We saw no harm in leasing land until Rafi' ibn Khadij told us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"4258","Usayd ibn Zuhayr from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi, from Qabisa ibn Uqba, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Usayd ibn Zuhayr, who said: Rafi' ibn Khadij came to us and mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqala, and said: Whoever has land and is in no need of it, let him grant it to his brother. And he forbade muzabana."
"4259","Al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Abd al-A'la al-Bawsi al-San'ani, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Qariz, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah said: The cupper and the cupped have broken the fast."
"4260","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Qariz, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earnings of the cupper are foul, and the dowry of the prostitute is foul, and the price of the dog is foul."
"4261","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Salih al-Shirazi, from Hajjaj ibn Nusayr, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Qariz, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earnings of the cupper are foul, and the dowry of the prostitute is foul, and the price of the dog is foul."
"4262","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Hamduwayh al-Saffar, from Affan, from Aban ibn Yazid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Qariz, from al-Sa'ib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earnings of the cupper are foul, and the dowry of the prostitute is foul, and the price of the dog is foul."
"4263","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki, from Hatim ibn Isma'il, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-A'raj, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Evil earnings are the dowry of the prostitute, and the price of the dog, and the earnings of the cupper."
"4264","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from his father, (ha) and narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, (ha) and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, all saying: from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, who said: I heard al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Evil earnings are the dowry of the prostitute, and the price of the dog, and the earnings of the cupper."
"4265","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jaban al-Jundaysaburi, from al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, from al-Fadl ibn Musa, from Ju'ayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid ibn Khusayfa, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he heard the Messenger of Allah saying: The worst earnings are the earnings of the cupper, and the price of the dog, and the dowry of the prostitute."
"4266","Salim ibn Abdillah ibn Umar from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma', from Juwayriya ibn Asma', from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, that Salim ibn Abdillah told him - and he asked him about leasing farms - and said: Rafi' ibn Khadij told Abdillah ibn Umar that his two uncles, who had attended Badr, told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Abdillah ibn Umar abandoned leasing them, though he used to lease them before that. Al-Zuhri said: So I said to Salim: Do you lease it? He said: Yes. I said: Then where is the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij? Salim said: Rafi' was excessive against himself (overstated)."
"4267","Narrated Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from his father, from Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Abu Uways Abdillah ibn Abdillah, from al-Zuhri, who said: I asked Salim ibn Abdillah about leasing farms. He said: Rafi' ibn Khadij told me, in the presence of Abdillah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms."
"4268","Narrated Ahmad ibn Tahir ibn Harmala ibn Yahya, from his grandfather, from Idris ibn Yahya al-Khawlani: Haywa ibn Shurayh told me, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Salim told me that his father used to do that until it reached him that Rafi' ibn Khadij said: The Messenger of Allah forbade leasing land."
"4269","Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, (ha) and narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, (ha) and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Ja'far al-Khatmi, who said: I came to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and said: Something has reached us from you about sharecropping. He said: Ibn Umar saw no harm in it, until Rafi' ibn Khadij mentioned to him a hadith about it. So he went to Rafi', and Rafi' told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Banu Haritha and saw crops in the land of Zuhayr, and said: How fine are the crops of Zuhayr! They said: They do not belong to Zuhayr. He said: Is it not the land of Zuhayr? They said: Yes indeed, but it is the crops of so-and-so. He said: Then return to him his expenses and take your crops. Rafi' said: So we returned to him his expenses and took our crops."
"4270","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Anmati, from Yahya ibn Ma'in, from Marwan ibn Mu'awiya al-Fazari, from Abu Ja'far Umayr ibn Yazid al-Ansari, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4271","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, (ha) and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, from Malik ibn Ziyad al-Kufi, (ha) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, (ha) and narrated Abu Husayn al-Qadi, from Yahya al-Himmani, all saying: from Abu al-Ahwas, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqala and muzabana, and said: Only three cultivate: a man who has land and cultivates it, and a man who has been granted land and he cultivates it, and a man who hires with gold or silver."
"4272","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, from Hajjaj ibn Nusayr, from Hassan ibn Ibrahim al-Kirmani, the uncle of Atiyya ibn Atiyya, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: I was sitting with Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib when they mentioned that some people say: Allah has decreed everything except deeds. He said: By Allah, I never saw Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib angry with an anger severer than it, until he was about to stand. Then he calmed and said: Did they say it? By Allah, I have heard a hadith about them that suffices them as evil; woe to them, do they know? I said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu Muhammad, what is it? He said: He looked at me, with some of his anger calmed, and said: Rafi' ibn Khadij told me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There will be a people of my umma who disbelieve in Allah and in the Qur'an while they do not perceive, as the Jews and Christians disbelieved. He said: I said: May I be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah, how is that? He said: They affirm part of qadar (decree) and disbelieve in part of it. He said: I said: Then what do they say? He said: They say: Good is from Allah and evil is from Iblis; thus they affirm thereby the Book of Allah, and they disbelieve in the Qur'an after faith and knowledge. What my umma will meet from them of enmity, hatred, and disputation! Those are the heretics of this umma; in their time there will be the injustice of the ruler, and they will suffer injustice, wrong, and favoritism. Then Allah the Exalted will send a plague and destroy most of them. Then there will be the swallowing (khasf), and how few of them will escape; the believer that day, his joy little, his grief severe. Then there will be the transformation (maskh), and Allah the Exalted will transform most of those into monkeys and pigs. Then the Dajjal will emerge soon after that. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept until we wept for his weeping. We said: What makes you weep? He said: Out of mercy for them, the wretched; for among them is the devout worshipper, and among them is the striver, though they are not the first to hasten to this saying, and to be straitened by bearing it. Most of those of Banu Isra'il who perished, perished by denying qadar. I said: May I be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah, then tell me: how is faith in qadar? He said: You believe in Allah alone, and that none besides Him owns harm or benefit, and you believe in Paradise and Hell, and you know that Allah the Exalted created them before creating creation. Then He created creation and made of them whom He willed for Paradise, and whom He willed for Hell - justice that from Him. And each acts for what has been concluded for him, and he will end to what has been concluded from him. I said: Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth."
"4273","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa, from Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri, from Ibn Lahi'a, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4274","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, from Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri, from Ibn Lahi'a, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4275","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Sadaqa ibn Khalid, from Umar ibn Qays, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn al-Zubayr, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat a foreleg; when he finished, he passed his fingers over the wall, then prayed Asr and Maghrib without performing ablution."
"4276","Narrated Abu Aqil Anas ibn Salm al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Asbagh al-Harrani Abd al-Aziz ibn Yahya, from Isa ibn Yunus, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Abu Awn al-Ansari, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Prophet forbade leasing and buying the lands of the non-Arabs (al-a'ajim)."
"4277","Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Humran: my father told me, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from al-Aswad ibn al-Ala', from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade muhaqala and muzabana."
"4278","Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari, from Amr ibn Abi Asim: my father told me, from Uthman ibn Murra, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"4279","Narrated Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb al-Nasa'i, from Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khali al-Himsi: my father told me, from Salama ibn Abd al-Malik al-Awdi, from al-Hasan ibn Salih, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting (of the hand) for fruit nor for kathar (palm pith)."
"4280","Sulayman ibn Yasar from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Sulayman ibn Harb, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, who said: Ya'la ibn Hakim wrote to me - and I heard it from him afterwards - who said: I asked Sulayman ibn Yasar, and he said, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to muhaqala land, leasing it for a third and a fourth and specified food. Then he commanded us regarding land to cultivate it or to have it cultivated, and he disliked leasing it and whatever besides that."
"4281","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, who said: Ya'la ibn Hakim wrote to me: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar narrating from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to muhaqala land, leasing it for a third and a fourth and specified food. Then one of my uncles came to me and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has today forbidden us a matter that was beneficial to us, and obedience to Allah and His Messenger is more beneficial. We used to muhaqala land, leasing it for a third and a fourth and specified food. So he forbade us to lease it for a fourth and a third and specified food, and he commanded the owner of the land to cultivate it or to have it cultivated."
"4282","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, from Ayyub, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to muhaqala land in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leasing it for a third and a fourth and specified food. Then one day a man of my uncles came to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden us a matter that was beneficial to us, and obedience to Allah and His Messenger is more beneficial to us. He has forbidden us to muhaqala land, to lease it for a third and a fourth and specified food, and he has commanded the owner of the land to cultivate it or to have it cultivated, and he disliked leasing it and whatever besides that."
"4283","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, from Abda ibn Sulayman, (ha) and narrated Ishaq ibn Khalawayh al-Wasiti, from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi, from Yazid ibn Zuray', both saying: from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to muhaqala in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and muhaqala is that a man gives his land for a third or a fourth or for specified food. Then one of my uncles came to us and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden us a matter that was beneficial to us, and obedience to Allah and obedience to His Messenger is more beneficial to us. We said: What is that? He said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or let his brother cultivate it, and let him not lease it for a third or a fourth, nor for specified food."
"4284","Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Ahmad ibn Isa al-Misri, from Ibn Wahb: Jarir ibn Hazim told me, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to muhaqala in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and muhaqala is that a man leases his land for a third or a fourth or for specified food. Then one of his uncles came and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden us a matter that was beneficial to us, and obedience to Allah and His Messenger is more beneficial to us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or let his brother cultivate it, and let him not lease it for a third and a fourth, nor for specified food."
"4285","Mahmud ibn Labid al-Ansari from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Nu'aym, from Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray Fajr at isfar (when dawn light spreads), for it is greater in reward - or: for your reward."
"4286","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri and Ibn Uyayna, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray the morning prayer at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4287","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti, from his father, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Muhammad ibn Jariya, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray Subh in the morning light (asbihu bi-al-subh), for it is greater in reward."
"4288","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz, from Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi, from Shu'ba, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Pray Subh at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4289","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Na'ila al-Asbahani, from Muhammad ibn al-Mughira, from al-Nu'man ibn Abd al-Salam, from Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq and Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray the Subh prayer at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4290","Narrated al-Abbas ibn Hamdan al-Hanafi al-Asbahani, from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi, from Husayn ibn Ali, from Za'ida, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4291","Narrated Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Yazid ibn Iyad, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worker over charity, with truth, is like the warrior in the cause of Allah until he returns."
"4292","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Yazid ibn Zuray', from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray Subh at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4293","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil, from Ibrahim ibn Rashid al-Adami: Mu'alla ibn Abd al-Rahman told us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray Subh at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4294","Narrated Abu Ma'n Thabit ibn Nu'aym al-Hawji, from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, from Shu'ba, from Abu Dawud, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give light with Fajr (nawwiru bi-al-fajr), for it is greater in reward."
"4295","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti, from his father, from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, from Shu'ba ibn al-Hajjaj: Dawud al-Nasri told me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Pray the Fajr prayer at isfar, for it is greater in reward."
"4296","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Qattan al-Misri, from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Mutarrif, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from a man of the Ansar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whenever you pray Fajr at isfar, it is greater in reward."
"4297","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti, from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi', from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us among Banu Abd al-Ashhal and prayed Maghrib with us in our mosque, then said: Pray these two rak'as in your houses."
"4298","Narrated Musa ibn Harun, from al-Haytham ibn Kharija, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah the Exalted loves a servant, He protects him from the world, as one of you keeps protecting his sick one from water."
"4299","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi, (ha) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both saying: from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Abdillah ibn Yazid al-Bakri, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: Nu'ayman said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a severe feverish ailment. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where are you, O Nu'ayman, from Mahya'a - and it was an unhealthy land."
"4300","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam, from Isma'il ibn Ja'far, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worker over charity, with truth, is like the warrior in the cause of Allah until he returns to his house."
"4301","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The worker over charity is like the warrior until he returns to his house."
"4302","Narrated Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi, from Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The worker over charity, with truth, is like the warrior in the cause of Allah."
"4303","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, from Abdillah ibn Shabib, from Isma'il ibn Abi Uways: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad told me, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thing I fear most for you is minor shirk. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is minor shirk? He said: Showing off (riya'); it will be said to whoever does that, when people come with their deeds: Go to those whom you used to show off to, and seek that with them."
"4304","Nafi', the freed slave of Ibn Umar, from Rafi'. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Arim Abu al-Nu'man, (ha) and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Sulayman ibn Harb, both saying: from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar used to lease his farms in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman and the early part of the rule of Mu'awiya (may Allah be pleased with them). Then a man came to him and said: Rafi' ibn Khadij claims that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. Nafi' said: So Ibn Umar went to Rafi', and I went with him. Ibn Umar said: What is it that has reached me from you, that you mention from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about leasing farms? He said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Abdillah, when asked after that about leasing farms, would say: Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"4305","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, (ha) and narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Yazid ibn Zuray', both saying: from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Rafi' ibn Khadij was narrating to Abdillah ibn Umar that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid leasing farms. So Ibn Umar abandoned it, and when asked about it he would say: Rafi' claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"4306","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar used to lease farms. It reached him that Rafi' ibn Khadij was transmitting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith. So he went out to him at al-Balat and asked him. Rafi' told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Abdillah abandoned leasing them."
"4307","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Qattat al-Kufi, from Minjab ibn al-Harith, from Ali ibn Mushir, from Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that he used to lease farms of his until Rafi' ibn Khadij told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Ibn Umar abandoned it."
"4308","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, from Abdillah ibn Salih: al-Layth told me: Kathir ibn Farqad told me, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to lease farms until it reached him that Rafi' ibn Khadij was transmitting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade that. Nafi' said: So Abdillah ibn Umar went out to him at al-Balat, and I was with him, and asked him. Rafi' said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Abdillah abandoned that."
"4309","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri, from Zakariyya ibn Yahya, scribe of al-Umari: al-Mufaddal ibn Faddala told us, from Abdillah ibn Sulayman al-Tawil, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to lease farms, and that Rafi' ibn Khadij was transmitting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade that. Nafi' said: So Abdillah went out to him on al-Balat, and I was with him, and asked him. Rafi' said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms. So Abdillah abandoned leasing them."
"4310","Narrated Musa ibn Harun, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma', from Juwayriya, from Nafi', that Rafi' ibn Khadij al-Ansari told Abdillah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms."
"4311","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin, from Ahmad ibn Salih, from Ibn Wahb: Amr ibn al-Harith told me, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah: Nafi' told me that he heard Ibn Umar saying: We used to lease our land until we heard the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4312","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, from Sa'id ibn Amr, from Abthar, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sharecropping (muzara'a)."
"4313","Narrated Abu Husayn al-Qadi, from Yahya al-Himmani, from Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that he used to sharecrop his land. Then he met Rafi' ibn Khadij, and Rafi' told him and forbade him that, and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms."
"4314","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Qasri at Qasr Ibn Hubayra, from Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti, from Abu Khulayd Utba ibn Hammad, from Ibn Thawban, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms."
"4315","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin, from Abd al-A'la ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Kala'i, from Zayn ibn Shu'ayb, from Usama ibn Zayd, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), when he heard the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land - Ibn Umar said: We used to lease it only for the rabi' of the water-carrier (the portion irrigated by his labor) and for some of what comes out of it of straw."
"4316","Narrated Bishran ibn Abd al-Malik al-Mawsili, from Ghassan ibn al-Rabi', from Thabit ibn Yazid, from Burd ibn Sinan, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them): that he used to hire out his land until Rafi' ibn Khadij told him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land. So he abandoned that."
"4317","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Jahm al-Simmari, from al-Walid ibn Amr ibn Sukayn: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Sil'i told us: Ibn Awn told us, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to take the lease of land until Rafi' ibn Khadij told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"4318","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both saying: from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Yahya ibn Hamza: al-Awza'i told me: Hafs ibn Inan told me, from Nafi', freed slave of Ibn Umar, that he told him, he said: Abdillah ibn Umar used to lease his land for some of what comes out of it. It reached him that Rafi' ibn Khadij mentions otherwise, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that. He said: We used to lease land before we knew the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij. Then he felt unease, and placed his hand on my shoulder until we were pushed to Rafi' ibn Khadij. Abdillah ibn Umar said to him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding leasing land? Rafi' said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - otherwise may Allah blind these two - saying: Do not lease land for anything."
"4319","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari, from Hakim ibn Sayf al-Harrani, from Ubaydillah ibn Amr, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"4320","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi', that Rafi' ibn Khadij told Abdillah ibn Umar - while he was leaning on my two hands - that his uncles came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then they returned to Rafi' after they narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing farms."
"4321","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan, from Suwayd ibn Amr al-Kalbi, from Zuhayr, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he used to lease his land until Rafi' met him and told him of the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that. Nafi' said: And I was with Ibn Umar when he asked Rafi'."
"4322","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, from Wahb ibn Baqiyya, from Khalid, from Abdillah, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to lease farms until Rafi' ibn Khadij told him, from some of his uncles, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land. So Ibn Umar abandoned it."
"4323","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Sawra, from al-Haytham ibn Kharija, both saying: from Muhammad ibn Himyar, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla: Aban ibn Salih told us, from Nafi', who said: I went out with Tawus to Rafi' ibn Khadij, and Tawus asked him about leasing land. He said: We used to give land for a half and a third, for what is in al-rabi' (irrigated produce) and for what is in al-ba'l (unirrigated produce). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that. When he turned back, Tawus struck my hands and said: If you have land - then he mentioned the hadith."
"4324","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, from Abdillah ibn Salih: al-Layth told me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he did not cease leasing his land until the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij reached him. So he came to him and asked him, and he told him. So Abdillah ibn Umar abandoned leasing it."
"4325","Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im from Rafi' ibn Khadij. Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf, from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, from Muhammad ibn Ja'far, from Utba ibn Muslim, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared sacred what is between the two lava tracts of Madina."
"4326","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from al-Qa'nabi, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Utba ibn Muslim, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared sacred what is between the two lava tracts of Madina."
"4327","Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman ibn Affan from Rafi'. Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, from Abdillah ibn Salih: al-Layth told me: Ibn al-Had told me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned Makka - saying: Ibrahim declared Makka sacred, and I declare sacred what is between its two lava tracts, meaning Madina."
"4328","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, from his father, both saying: from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, from Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned Makka - saying: Ibrahim (peace be upon him) declared Makka sacred, and I declare sacred what is between its two lava tracts, meaning Madina."
"4329","Narrated Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri, from his father, from Ibn Abi Hazim, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, from Abdillah ibn Uthman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned Makka - saying: Ibrahim declared Makka sacred, and I declare sacred what is between its two lava tracts, for Madina."
"4330","Narrated Abu Husayn al-Qadi, from Yahya al-Himmani, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4331","Hanzala ibn Qays from Rafi'. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Qa'nabi, (ha) and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl, from Abdillah ibn Yusuf, (ha) and narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani, from Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, all of them from Malik, from Rabi'a, from Hanzala ibn Qays, that he asked Rafi' ibn Khadij about leasing land. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land. I said: For gold and silver? He said: As for gold and silver, there is no harm."
"4332","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, from Abdillah ibn Salih: al-Layth told me, from Rabi'a, from Hanzala ibn Qays, that he asked Rafi' ibn Khadij about leasing land. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us leasing land for what comes out of it."
"4333","Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi, from Abu Hudhayfa, from Sufyan, from Rabi'a, from Hanzala ibn Qays, who said: I asked Rafi' ibn Khadij about leasing white (uncultivated) land for gold and silver. He said: No harm; they were only forbidden al-armath (uncertain produce-shares)."
"4334","Narrated Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Maqdisi, from Muhammad ibn Kathir al-San'ani: al-Awza'i told us, from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to hire out our land for al-madhayanat (water-channels) and the ends of the streams; then this would be safe and this would perish. So we were forbidden that, and there is no harm in a specified wage."
"4335","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi, from his father, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, from his father, from Musa ibn A'yan, both saying: from al-Awza'i, from Rabi'a, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to lease land in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for al-madhayanat and the ends of the streams; then this would perish and this would be safe. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us that."
"4336","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri, from Abd al-A'la ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Kala'i, from Zayn ibn Shu'ayb, from Abu Ma'dan Amir ibn Murra al-Makki, from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to lease land, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us that."
"4337","Narrated Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri, from his father, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Rabi'a, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to lease land in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the rabi' of the water-carrier and al-madhayanat and a portion of straw. The Prophet disliked that."
"4338","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, from Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: Hanzala ibn Qays told me, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to lease our land; and there was at that time neither gold nor silver. We used to lease our land for a fourth and for the unknown; then perhaps this would perish and this would be safe."
"4339","Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath, from Hammad ibn Salama, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We, Banu Harith, were the people of Madina with the most fields. We used to lease land and stipulate upon the tenants that what the madhayanat and al-rabi' watered is ours, and what the streams watered is yours. Then perhaps this would perish and this would be safe, and perhaps this would be safe and this would perish. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that; and we had at that time neither gold nor silver, so we knew that."
"4340","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa, from al-Humaydi, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: Hanzala ibn Qays al-Zuraqi told me, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid leasing it - meaning land - for gold and silver. But we used to lease it, then cultivate, with this side for us and this side for them; what Allah the Exalted brought forth from this is for this one, and what Allah the Exalted brought forth from this is for this one. So he forbade us that."
"4341","Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban from Rafi'. Narrated Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting (of the hand) for fruit nor for kathar (palm pith)."
"4342","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Nu'aym, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4343","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisī, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam, (ha) and narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Qa'nabi, (ha) and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati, from Abdillah ibn Yusuf, all of them from Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, that a slave stole a young palm-shoot from the walled garden of a man and planted it in the walled garden of his master. The owner of the shoot went out seeking his shoot and took it. He sought judgment against the slave from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who imprisoned the slave and wanted to cut his hand. The master of the slave went to Rafi' ibn Khadij and asked him about that. He told that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4344","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Yusuf al-Qadi, both saying: from Musaddad, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4345","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, from Musaddad, from Abd al-Warith, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4346","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, from his father, (ha) and narrated Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami, from Ahmad ibn Yunus, both saying: from Zuhayr, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4347","Narrated Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani, from Abdillah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi, from Ubaydillah ibn Amr, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4348","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari, from Sa'id ibn Hafs al-Nufayli, from Yunus ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4349","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi, from Mu'awiya ibn Amr, from Mansur ibn Kulthum, from Za'ida, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4350","Narrated Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar."
"4351","Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali al-Madani narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us: Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no amputation (of the hand) for (stealing) fruit nor for kathar (the soft pith/spadix of the palm)."
"4352","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no amputation (of the hand) for (stealing) fruit nor for kathar."
"4353","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no amputation (of the hand) for (stealing) fruit nor for kathar."
"4354","Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from a paternal aunt of his, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no amputation (of the hand) for (stealing) fruit nor for kathar."
"4355","Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tabba' narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Mujahid: Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us, and the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is upon the head and the eye (i.e. fully accepted); he forbade us to work the land for a share of its produce or for coined silver, and he forbade us the earnings of the cupper."
"4356","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from Rafi', from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he passed by a walled garden (orchard) which pleased him, and said: To whom does this belong? I (Rafi') said: It is mine. He said: How did you come by it? I said: I leased it. He said: Do not lease it for anything."
"4357","Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Jabbarah ibn Mughallis and 'Ubadah ibn Ziyad narrated to us, both saying: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Qays ibn Rifa'ah, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us, and the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is upon the head and both eyes; he forbade us to lease out our land for a share of what comes forth from it or for coined silver, and he said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it himself or let him lend it (for free use) to his brother."
"4358","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us: he forbade us, when one of us had land, to give it out for a share of its yield, a third or a half, and he said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or let him lend it (for free use) to his brother."
"4359","Muhammad ibn Mahan al-Ubulli narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Shahidi narrated to us: 'Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, who said: I took Tawus by the hand until I brought him in to Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, and he narrated to him, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he forbade leasing out white (i.e. uncultivated/open) land."
"4360","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us: my father narrated to me: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah: that he forbade leasing out land."
"4361","Al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: My two maternal uncles came to us in the evening and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us, but obedience to Allah and obedience to His Messenger is more beneficial to us. He said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or let him lend it (for free use) to his brother."
"4362","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Dharr, from Mujahid, from Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, who said: Rafi' came to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us a matter that was gentle (easy) for us, and obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is gentler for us; he forbade us to cultivate land except land whose ownership one of us owns outright, or a free loan (of land) which a man lends."
"4363","Abu Khalifah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid: that Usayd ibn Zuhayr said: When one of us had no need of his land he would give it out for a third, a quarter and a half, stipulating for himself a third (of the produce of) the water-channels, the reapers, and what the spring rains watered. He said: We used to work with iron (tools) and whatever Allah willed, and we got benefit from it, until Rafi' ibn Khadij came to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbids you a matter that was beneficial to you, but obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more beneficial to you: when one of you has no need of his land, let him lend it (for free use) to his brother or leave it, and he forbids you muzabanah (buying dates on the tree for harvested dates). Muzabanah is when there is a large amount of dates on the palms and one says: I have taken it for such-and-such a number of wasqs of dates."
"4364","Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani narrated to us: Abu Kurayb narrated to us: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from al-Mufaddal ibn Mulahhil, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Usayd ibn Zuhayr, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabanah."
"4365","Zakariyya ibn Hamdawayh al-Baghdadi narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Zubaydi, from Mujahid, from Usayd, the nephew of Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you has no need of his land, let him lend it (for free use) to his brother or leave it; and he forbade muzabanah."
"4366","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from his father, from al-Hakam, from a man, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us muhaqalah (leasing land for a share of produce) and muzabanah."
"4367","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us al-haql (leasing land for a share). Al-haql is (for) a third and a quarter."
"4368","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn 'Abdus narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah informed us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah came out to us and forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us, but the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is better. He said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or lend it (for free use) or leave it. That was mentioned to Tawus, and he said: Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) was more knowledgeable. He said: Ibn 'Abbas said: For a man to lend his land to his brother (for free use) is better for him."
"4369","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Hafs ibn 'Amr al-Rabbali narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abd al-Majid narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the land of a man of the Ansar who was cultivating, and he knew that he was needy, and said: To whom does this belong? He said: To so-and-so; he gave it to me for rent. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If only he had lent it (for free use) to his brother."
"4370","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Hafs ibn 'Umar al-Rabbali narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abd al-Majid narrated to us: Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sa'id, from Mujahid, who said: I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij say: My paternal uncles went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then returned to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden you a matter that was beneficial to us, but obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more beneficial to you. He said: Let one of you cultivate his land, or lend it (for free use), or leave it fallow."
"4371","Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Bara' narrated to us: al-Mu'afa ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that he came to his people and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden you a matter that was easy for you, but obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what is hard for you is better than disobeying him in what was easy for you; he has forbidden you to give out your land for a third and a quarter, but let him cultivate it or have his brother cultivate it. They said: Then it will lie fallow. He said: Leave it to lie fallow."
"4372","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Musa ibn Sufyan al-Jundayshaburi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn 'Asim narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Mujahid, who said: I came in to Tawus and narrated to him what I had heard from Rafi' ibn Khadij of the prohibition of muhaqalah, and Tawus struck my chest and said: You narrate to me from Rafi', yet Mu'adh ibn Jabal acted upon it (i.e. sharecropping) in the time of the Messenger of Allah, giving out land for a half and a third?"
"4373","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibn Shihab, from Usayd ibn Rafi', from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to lease out land for a share of what is in it."
"4374","Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bukayr narrated to him, that Usayd ibn Rafi' narrated to him, that Rafi' came to his clan and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a matter that was easy for us, but in the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there is blessing and guidance. They said: What is that? He said: Al-haql (leasing land). I said to Usayd: What is al-haql? He said: Leasing out land. I said: How did they lease it? He said: For a fourth and for a portion of the harvest."
"4375","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us: 'Utbah ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Qays narrated to him, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am the one of the Ansar with the most land. He said: Cultivate it. I said: It is more than that. He said: Then leave it fallow."
"4376","Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami' al-Misri narrated to us: Abu Tahir ibn al-Sarh narrated to us: Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ayyub, from Sahl ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him and called him, so he came out to him and walked with him until he reached the mosque; then he left, performed ghusl, and returned, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the traces of ghusl on him and asked him about his ghusl. He said: I heard your call while I was with my wife, so I rose before finishing and performed ghusl. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Water is (required) on account of water (i.e. ghusl is for ejaculation). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said afterwards: When the circumcised part passes beyond the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory."
"4377","Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah al-Bazzaz al-Tustari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Yusuf al-Miski narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya'la Zanbur narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Ubaydah, from 'Isa ibn Sahl ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqalah, muzabanah and munabadhah (a sale by mere throwing/touching), and he forbade a man to say to a man: Buy this for cash and I will buy it (from you) on credit before he buys it and takes possession of it; and (he forbade) kali' for kali' (debt for debt) and debt for debt."
"4378","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us: Harami ibn 'Umarah narrated to me: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Nufay' ibn 'Ali al-Kilabi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command delaying the 'Asr prayer."
"4379","Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Sawwaf narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us: Rifa'ah ibn al-Hurayr narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not lie about me, for lying about me is not like lying about anyone else."
"4380","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Qirmiti al-Baghdadi narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Ya'qub al-'Uthmani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from Hurayr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: On the day of the Trench (Khandaq) there was no fortress more secure than the fortress of Banu Harith, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed the women, children and offspring in it, and said: If anyone comes upon you, brandish (flash) the sword. Then a man of Banu Tha'labah ibn Sa'd called Bujdan, of Banu Jahhash, came to them on a horse until he was at the foot of the fortress, and began saying to the women: Come down to me; it is better for you. They brandished the sword, and the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, so a group hastened to the fortress, among them a man of Banu Harithah called Zuhayr ibn Rafi'. He said: O Bujdan, come out (to fight). He came out to him, and he charged at him on his horse, killed him and took his head, and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4381","Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us: my father narrated to us: 'Uthman 'Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi narrated to us: Aban ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek (a good) neighbour before (seeking) the house, and (a good) companion before (setting out on) the road."
"4382","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah of Tihamah, and the people came upon camels and sheep, so they hastened and boiled them in the pots. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them and ordered the pots to be overturned, and he equated ten sheep to a camel. He said: A camel among them bolted, and a man shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These beasts have wild (untamed) ones like the wildness of wild animals; whatever overcomes you of them, do thus with it. Then Rafi' ibn Khadij came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear - or hope - that we will meet the enemy, and we have no knives with us; may we slaughter with reeds? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, except the tooth and the nail; and I shall tell you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians. Rafi' said: Then a water-carrying camel (nadih) fell into a well at Madinah and was slaughtered from the side of its shoulder-blade - meaning its flank - and 'Umar took a tenth of it (a foreleg share) for a dirham."
"4383","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Azhar ibn Marwan al-Raqashi narrated to us: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Sa'id, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we hope to meet our enemy, but may not have with us some of the equipment we need; may we eat every animal slaughtered with a reed? He said: Yes; everything that causes the blood to flow is a (valid) slaughter, except the tooth and the nail."
"4384","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: my father (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to me: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that he said: O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives with us. He said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, except the tooth and the nail. I shall tell you: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taken camels (as booty), and a camel among them bolted, and a man of the people shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These camels have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; if anything of them overcomes you, do thus with it. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make, in the division of booty, ten sheep (equal to) a camel. He said: And Sufyan narrated to me from him (the same). Muhammad said: And I have heard this wording from Sufyan."
"4385","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Za'idah narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah of Tihamah, the people grew hungry and came upon sheep and camels. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the rear of the people, so they hastened, slaughtered, and set up the pots. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them and ordered the pots to be overturned; then he divided (the booty), equating ten sheep to a camel. There was a camel among the people that bolted; they pursued it but it exhausted them, so a man aimed an arrow at it and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These beasts have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; whatever bolts from them against you, do thus with it. We said: We fear - or hope - that we will meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives with us; may we slaughter with reeds? He said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, except the tooth and the nail; and I shall inform you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4386","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and camels and sheep were taken (as booty). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the rear of the people, so they hastened, slaughtered, and set up the pots, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon them and ordered the pots to be overturned. Then he divided (the booty), equating ten sheep to a camel. There was a camel among the people that ran wildly; they pursued it but it exhausted them, so a man aimed an arrow at it and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These beasts have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; whatever bolts from them against you, do thus with it. My grandfather said: We hope - or fear - that we will meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives with us; may we slaughter with reeds? He said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah the Exalted's name is mentioned over it, eat it, except the tooth and the nail; and I shall inform you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4387","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his father, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives with us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, so long as it is not tooth nor nail; and I shall tell you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4388","Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamzah al-Dimashqi narrated to us: my father narrated to me, from his father: Dawud ibn 'Isa al-Kufi narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and we came upon camels and sheep. Some people went ahead in the van of the people, slaughtered, and set up their pots before (the booty) was divided. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by - he was at the rear of the people - and saw the pots set up, and said: What is this? It was said: O Messenger of Allah, they slaughtered and cooked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the pots to be overturned, then ordered the booty to be divided, placing ten sheep in place of every camel. He said: While we were upon that, a camel bolted; a man of the people pursued it, and a man shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These camels have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; whatever overcomes you of them, do thus with it. Then my grandfather said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear we shall meet the polytheists tomorrow, and we have no knives with us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, except tooth or nail; and I shall tell you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4389","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Hubab al-Himyari narrated to us: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that a camel of the charity camels bolted, and they pursued it, and when they despaired of catching it, a man shot it with an arrow and hit a vital spot. They asked him about eating it, and he commanded them to eat it, and said: It has wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; if you fear anything of them, do with it as you did with this one, then eat it."
"4390","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Hubayb ibn Habib narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were on a campaign with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man of the people said: O Messenger of Allah, we shall face the enemy in the morning and we have no knives with us; may we slaughter with reeds and sharp stones? He said: Yes; mention Allah's name over it and eat it, except the tooth and the nail; as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4391","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bazigh and Dahir ibn Nuh narrated to us, both saying: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4392","Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4393","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Khadij, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We came upon camels, and a camel among them bolted, so we shot it with arrows. We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: These camels have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; whatever bolts of them, do thus with it, and eat it."
"4394","Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us: Mindal ibn 'Ali, 'Umar ibn 'Ubayd, and Hammad ibn Shu'ayb al-Himmani narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and the people were struck by hunger, and we came upon camels and sheep. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the rear of the people, so the van of the people hastened, slaughtered and sacrificed, and set up the pots. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon them while the pots were boiling, and ordered them to be overturned; then he divided (the booty), equating ten sheep to a camel. A camel bolted; they pursued it but it exhausted them, so a man aimed an arrow at it and restrained it; and he mentioned the like of it."
"4395","Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us: Mubarak ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"4396","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Zayd ibn al-Huraysh narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, and we said: We shall face the enemy in the morning and we have no knives with us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything that causes the blood to flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it, so long as it is not tooth or nail; for the tooth is a bone, and the nail is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4397","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: 'Abd al-Warith ibn 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Layth narrated to us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his father, from his grandfather Rafi', who said: We came upon sheep and divided them at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and a camel among them bolted, and a man pursued it on his horse and stabbed it with a spear - or struck it with a sword. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These camels have wild ones like the wildness of wild animals; if anything of them bolts, do thus with it. A man said: We used to slaughter with sharp stones and split sticks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cause the blood to flow with whatever you wish, so long as it is not tooth or nail; for the tooth is a bone, and the nail is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"4398","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave to Abu Sufyan, Safwan ibn Umayyah, 'Uyaynah ibn Hisn and al-Aqra' ibn Habis - the hadith (continues)."
"4399","'Amr ibn Thawr al-Judhami narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is from the blast (fierce heat) of Hell, so cool it with water."
"4400","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Fever is from the outburst of Hell, so cool it with water."
"4401","Al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, and Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is from the blast of Hell, so extinguish it with cold water."
"4402","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us: Mus'ab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Fever is from the blast of Hell, so cool it with water."
"4403","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us: Mus'ab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to Ibn Nu'ayman and began saying: Remove the harm, Lord of mankind, God of mankind."
"4404","Ubayd al-'Ijli narrated to us: Jabbarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us: Hammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity closes seventy doors of evil."
"4405","Ubayd al-'Ijli narrated to us: Jabbarah ibn Mughallis narrated to us: Hammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand of the giver is the upper (hand), and the hand of the taker is the lower, until the Day of Resurrection."
"4406","'Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi narrated to us: Jabbarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Muslims are (bound) by their conditions, in that which is lawful."
"4407","'Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi and al-Hasan ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us, both saying: 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym, who said: I heard 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his grandfather: that when he died he left a slave-girl, a water-carrying camel (nadih), a slave cupper, and land. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning the slave-girl: He forbade her earnings; and he said concerning the cupper: Whatever he earns, feed it to the water-carrying camel; and he said concerning the land: Cultivate it."
"4408","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Balj, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, who said: Rifa'ah died in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving a slave cupper, a water-carrying camel, and land. He said: As for the cupper, do not eat from his earnings, but feed the water-carrying camel with it. They said: (What about) the slave-woman - she earns? He said: Do not eat from the earnings of the slave-woman, for I fear she may seek it through her private parts."
"4409","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us: Husayn ibn Numayr narrated to us: Abu Balj Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym narrated to us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his father, who said: My father died leaving land, a slave-girl, a slave cupper, and a water-carrying camel. They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that, and he said to them concerning the land: Cultivate it or lend it (for free use); and he forbade them the earnings of the slave-woman, and said: Feed the earnings of the cupper to the water-carrying camel."
"4410","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Shuja' ibn Makhlad and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us - the wording being Shuja's - both saying: Hushaym narrated to us: Abu Balj informed us, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah: that his grandfather died leaving a slave-woman who yielded revenue, and they mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he disliked the earnings of the slave-woman, saying: Perhaps she finds nothing and so seeks it through herself. Ibn Abi Shaybah said in his chain: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Balj, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' al-Ansari."
"4411","Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi narrated to us: Maymun ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Layth, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the crescent he would say: A crescent of good and right guidance. Then he would say: O Allah, I ask You of the good of this - three times - O Allah, I ask You of the good of this month and the good of the decree, and I seek refuge in You from its evil - three times."
"4412","Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Himsi narrated to us, and Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: 'Atiyyah ibn Baqiyyah narrated to me, and Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hanan al-Himsi narrated to us, and 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us - all saying: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Ibn Thawban narrated to us: Abu Mudrik narrated to me, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: Narrate (from me), but let whoever lies about me take his seat in Hell. I said: O Messenger of Allah, we hear things from you - may we write them? He said: Write, and there is no harm."
"4413","Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Hud al-Wasiti narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: al-Mas'udi informed us, from Wa'il ibn Dawud, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he was asked: Which earning is best? He said: A man's work with his hand, and every blessed sale."
"4414","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Rahawayh informed us: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: Jibril (peace be upon him) - or an angel - came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: How do you count the people of Badr among you? He said: They are the best of people. Jibril (peace be upon him) said: And likewise, those of the angels who witnessed Badr."
"4415","Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Qatirani narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Wasiti narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: A man of the Ansar was found slain at Khaybar, and his kin went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: Do you have two witnesses to testify against the killer of your companion? They said: O Messenger of Allah, there was no Muslim there; they are Jews, and they dare to do what is greater than this. He said: Then choose fifty of them and have them swear. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid the blood-money for him from his own funds."
"4416","Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, and 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, both saying: Abu Isma'il al-Mu'addib narrated to us: Hurayr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij al-Ansari narrated to us, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Bilal: Brighten (delay until brightness) the Fajr (prayer) so that the people can see the landing-spots of their arrows."
"4417","Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to us, saying: I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Brighten the Fajr so that the people can see the landing-spots of their arrows."
"4418","Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Nafi' al-Tahhan al-Misri and Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, both saying: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, and 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Musafir narrated to us, both saying: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Hurayr informed me, from his father, from his grandfather Rafi': that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was sowing (a field), and asked him about it, and he informed him that the land belonged to the family of Usayd, and he had leased it from them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to take his rent (what he had paid) from them and to return their land's rent-charge to them. The wording is Ahmad ibn Salih's. Ahmad ibn Salih said: Al-ita' is the value - the value of what comes forth."
"4419","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Shayban narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us: Rifa'ah ibn Hurayr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to us: my grandfather narrated to me, from his father, who said: I came, I and al-Khatmi, to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was heading for Badr, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I wish to go out with you. He kept closing his hand, saying: I consider you too young, and I do not know what you will do when you meet the enemy. I said: Do you know that I am the best shot of those who shoot? But he sent me back, and I did not witness Badr."
"4420","Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Ya'qub al-Talqani narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Yazid ibn Abi Shuja',: 'Isa ibn Sahl ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to us, who said: I was an orphan in the care of my grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, and I performed Hajj with him. My brother 'Imran ibn Sahl ibn Rafi' came to him and said to him: O my father, we have leased our land to so-and-so for two hundred dirhams. He said: O my son, leave that off, for Allah will give you other cultivation instead; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing out land."
"4421","Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us: Kharijah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sulayman ibn Zayd ibn Thabit narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Rafi', from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Affection (al-wudd) which is inherited among the people of Islam."
"4422","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madani narrated to us, and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, both saying: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Yahya ibn Ishaq, the nephew of Rafi', from Rafi': that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you lies down on his right side and then says: O Allah, I submit myself to You, I turn my back to You, I entrust my affair to You; there is no refuge from You except to You; and I believe, our Lord, in You and in Your messengers - if he dies that night, he enters Paradise."
"4423","'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, and Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Maqdisi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-San'ani narrated to us, and Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah al-Bablutti narrated to us - all saying: al-Awza'i narrated to us: Abu al-Najashi narrated to me: Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me, who said: We used to pray the 'Asr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we would slaughter a camel, divide it, cook it, and eat ripe (well-cooked) meat before the sun set."
"4424","Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah al-Bablutti narrated to us - all saying: al-Awza'i narrated to us: Abu al-Najashi narrated to me, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We used to pray Maghrib in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and one of us would leave and could still see the landing-spots of his arrows."
"4425","Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Awza'i: Abu al-Najashi 'Ata' ibn Suhayb, the freedman of Rafi' ibn Khadij, narrated to me, who said: I accompanied Rafi' ibn Khadij for six years, and he narrated to me, from his uncle Zuhayr ibn Rafi': that he met him one day and said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden us a matter that was beneficial to us. Rafi' said: I said: Whatever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has said is the truth. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you do with your muhaqalah (leased lands)? We said: We lease them for a quarter and for (a number of) wasqs of dates and barley. He said: Do not do so; cultivate them, or have them cultivated, or hold them."
"4426","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-'Azim al-'Anbari narrated to us: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us: Abu al-Najashi narrated to me: Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while they were pollinating the palms - meaning fertilizing them. He said: What are you doing? They said: We used to do it. He said: If you did not do it, it would be better. So they left them, and they produced poor (unfertilized) dates. That was mentioned to him, and he said: I am only a man; when I command you with something of your religion, take it, and when I command you with something of your worldly affairs, I am only a man."
"4427","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: my father (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to me: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij and Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah - both narrated to him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabanah; and muzabanah is (exchanging) dates (on the tree) for dates, except the people of 'araya (exempted small sales), for whom he gave permission."
"4428","Ibrahim al-Dimashqi narrated to us: my father narrated to us, and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: Duhaym narrated to us, both saying: Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling fresh dates (on the tree) for dried dates, but he permitted the 'ariyyah (exempted sale) for its people (owners) to buy it, by its estimated amount, for dates which they eat."
"4429","Muhammad ibn Fadalah al-Jawhari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd ibn Hisab narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah and Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning qasamah (the oath-taking procedure in murder cases)."
"4430","Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah and Rafi' ibn Khadij, both saying: 'Abdullah ibn Sahl ibn Zayd and Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud went out, until when they were at Khaybar they separated in some business there, and 'Abdullah ibn Sahl was found slain. Muhayyisah buried him, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he and Huwayyisah, the two sons of Mas'ud, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl. 'Abd al-Rahman began to speak, though he was the youngest of the people, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The elder (first). So his two companions spoke, and he spoke along with them. Then they mentioned to him the killing of 'Abdullah ibn Sahl, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you swear fifty (oaths) that he is the killer of your companion? They said: How can we swear upon what we did not witness? He said: Then the Jews will clear themselves from you with fifty oaths. They said: How can we accept the oaths of a disbelieving people? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid the blood-money for him when he saw that."
"4431","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu Umayyah al-Ansari, from 'Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah al-Zuraqi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I came in one day to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while they had a pot boiling with meat. A piece of fat pleased me, so I took it and swallowed it, and I complained (of stomach pain) from it for a year. Then I mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: In it was the breath (effect) of seven persons. Then he wiped my belly, and I vomited it up green; and by the One who sent him with the truth, I have not complained of my belly until this hour."
"4432","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Ghiyath ibn Ibrahim al-Kufi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah narrated to us, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: I saw a thread in the hand of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I said: What is this? He said: I remind myself with it."
"4433","Muhammad ibn 'Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us: 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn 'Asim narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, the freedman of Banu Tamim, narrated to me, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to tie a thread in his ring to remind himself with it."
"4434","Ibrahim ibn Hisham al-Baghawi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Shadhakuni narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate a (shoulder) limb (of meat) then prayed without performing ablution."
"4435","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Jumhur narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Nuh narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: Delegations of the Arabs came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but no delegation came to us harder-hearted, nor more likely to have Islam unsettled in their hearts, than Banu Hanifah."
"4436","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Jumhur narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Nuh narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: Al-Rajjal ibn Ghanmawayh had such humility, devotion to reciting the Qur'an, and goodness, in the view of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as was astonishing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day, with al-Rajjal sitting with us among a group, and said: One of this group is in the Fire. Rafi' said: I looked among the people, and there were Abu Hurayrah al-Dawsi, Abu Arwa al-Dawsi, al-Tufayl ibn 'Amr al-Dawsi, and Rajjal ibn Ghanmawayh; I kept looking in amazement, saying: Who is this wretched one? When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, Banu Hanifah apostatized, and I asked: What happened to al-Rajjal ibn Ghanmawayh? They said: He was tempted (fell away) - he is the one who testified for Musaylimah, against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he (Musaylimah) was made a partner with him in his affair after him. I said: Whatever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said is the truth. Al-Rajjal was heard saying: Two rams butted; the dearer of them to us is our ram."
"4437","Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr al-Dayri, from Ja'far ibn Miqlas, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Badr: By the One in whose hand is my soul, if a newborn were born among the people of religion, acting in all obedience to Allah and avoiding all sins, deeply versed (in worship) for forty years, until returned to the feeblest old age, or returned to knowing nothing after knowledge, not one of you would reach (the rank of) this night. And he said: The angels who witnessed Badr have superiority over those of them who stayed behind."
"4438","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu 'Ufayr al-Ansari: that Rafi' ibn Khadij used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to lease out the mahaqil (lands leased for a share); and al-mahaqil are extra (surplus) portions of land."
"4439","Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, and Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shaybah al-Juddi narrated to us, and 'Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us: Abu Bilal al-Ash'ari narrated to us, and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, and Abu Khalifah narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, and 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Uzayz al-Mawsili narrated to us: Ghassan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, and Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us - all saying: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever sows in the land of a people without their permission has nothing of the crop, and his expenses are returned to him."
"4440","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abi 'Ubaydullah narrated to us: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us: 'Abbad ibn Mansur narrated to us, from 'Ata', who said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to dislike leasing land for a third and a quarter when there were palms in it, and he used to lease white (open) land for gold and silver. Rafi' ibn Khadij met him and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid leasing out land, and forbid muhaqalah and muzabanah."
"4441","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, 'Ata' and Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and forbade us a matter that was beneficial to us, but the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is better for us than what he forbade us. He said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it, or have his brother cultivate it, or lend it (for free use). Shu'bah said: 'Abd al-Malik used to combine (the narrations of) Tawus, 'Ata' and Mujahid. Shu'bah said: As though the one he narrates from is Mujahid. Shu'bah said: The master of the hadith."
"4442","Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, both saying: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from 'Ata', from Iyas ibn Khalifah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) told 'Ammar to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhy (pre-seminal fluid). He said: He should wash his private parts and perform ablution."
"4443","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Hafs ibn 'Amr al-Rabbali narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Nafi' al-Makki narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from 'Ata', from Iyas ibn Khalifah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about madhy, and he said: Wash your penis and perform your ablution for prayer."
"4444","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: al-Hakam ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm narrated to us, who said: I heard my father mention, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he forbade muzara'ah (sharecropping)."
"4445","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: Bukayr ibn 'Amir al-Bajali narrated to us: Ibn Abi Nu'm narrated to us: Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to us: that he cultivated a land, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was watering it, and said: To whom do the crop and the land belong? He said: My crop, by my hand and work, for half, and half for the sons of so-and-so. He said: You have dealt in usury (you have wronged); return the land to its people and take your expenses."
"4446","'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Shu'bah informed us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mankind is one party, and my companions are a party. Then Zayd ibn Thabit and Rafi' ibn Khadij said: He has spoken the truth - while they were with Marwan ibn al-Hakam."
"4447","'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Dabbi al-Basri narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Hayyan, from his brother Muqatil ibn Hayyan, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-'Aliyah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not rise from a gathering until he said: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. Then he would say: It is an expiation for what occurs in the gathering."
"4448","'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us: Khalid informed us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from al-Qasim ibn 'Asim, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a crop and said: To whom does this crop belong? They said: To so-and-so, and the land to so-and-so. He forbade that, and said: Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or lend it (for free use) to his brother."
"4449","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hammad al-Shu'aythi narrated to us: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing out land."
"4450","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us: Abu Hilal narrated to us, who said: I asked Muhammad ibn Sirin about leasing out land, and he said: Rafi' ibn Khadij said: Our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us - or: the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade - leasing out land."
"4451","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from 'Ata', from a man of Banu Harithah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw on our mounts cloaks with broad red threads, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do I not see redness has overcome you? He said: We rose hastily at the word of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until some of our camels shied, and we took the cloaks and stripped them off them."
"4452","'Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Raddad al-'Amiri narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from 'Amrah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that he was sitting at the pulpit of Marwan ibn al-Hakam at Makkah, with Marwan addressing the people. Marwan mentioned Makkah and its merit, but did not mention Madinah. Rafi' felt displeasure at that in himself - he had grown old - so he rose to him and said: O you speaker, I see you have dwelt long on Makkah and mentioned of its merit, and what you have left unmentioned of its merit is greater; yet you have not mentioned Madinah. I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Madinah is better than Makkah."
"4453","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from 'Uthman ibn Zufar, from one of the sons of Rafi' ibn Makith, from Rafi' ibn Makith - who was among those who witnessed al-Hudaybiyyah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good treatment of those under one's authority is growth (a blessing), bad character is ill omen, righteousness increases lifespan, and charity prevents an evil death."
"4454","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Ansari al-Asbahani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed al-'Aqabah: Rafi' ibn Malik al-Zuraqi."
"4455","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me: Ma'bad ibn Ka'b ibn Malik ibn al-Qayn, of Banu Salamah, informed me, from his brother 'Ubaydullah, from his father Ka'b ibn Malik, who said: We went out in the Hajj in which we pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-'Aqabah, and the naqib (chief) of Banu Zurayq was Rafi' ibn Malik ibn al-'Ajlan."
"4456","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: 'Arim narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: Mu'adh ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' narrated to me: Rifa'ah was a Badri, and Rafi' ibn Malik was of the people of al-'Aqabah and did not witness Badr."
"4457","Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Khalid ibn Hayyan al-Raqqi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Malik, who said: I heard my father say: Jibril said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How are the people of Badr among you? He said: They are our best. Jibril said: And those of the angels who witnessed Badr are our best."
"4458","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Mushma'ill ibn Iyas say: I heard 'Amr ibn Sulaym al-Muzani say: I heard Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Muzani say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The tree (the lote-tree / the 'ajwah palm) and the 'ajwah (dates of Madinah) are from Paradise. Abu al-Qasim said: He is al-Mushma'ill ibn 'Amr ibn Iyas."
"4459","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us: al-Mushma'ill ibn 'Amr narrated to us, and 'Amr ibn Sulaym al-Muzani narrated to me, who said: I heard Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Muzani say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - while I was a young servant-boy - say: The tree and the 'ajwah are from Paradise."
"4460","'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to me: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to me. And Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us. And 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us - all saying: Hilal ibn 'Amir al-Muzani narrated to us, from Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Muzani, who said: I came forward with my father while I was a boy - Yahya ibn Sa'id said in his hadith: a young servant, or above that; and Ya'la said: five or six years old - in the Farewell Hajj, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was addressing the people upon a grey mule, with 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) conveying (repeating) from him, the people between sitting and standing. My father sat down, and I slipped through the mounts until I reached the mule; I took hold of its stirrup, placed my hand upon his knee and wiped up to the leg until I reached the foot with it, then I put my palm between the sandal and the foot - and to this hour it seems to me I still feel the coolness of his feet upon my palm. The wording is al-Umawi's hadith."
"4461","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi and 'Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, both saying: 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us - all saying: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Ibn Abi al-Hakam al-Ghifari narrated to us: my grandfather narrated to me, from the uncle of my father, Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Ghifari, who said: I used to pelt the date-palms of the Ansar while I was a boy, and they brought me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O boy, why do you pelt the palms? I said: I eat! He said: Do not pelt the palms, but eat of what falls at their bases. Then he wiped my head and said: O Allah, fill his belly."
"4462","Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us: Mu'adh ibn Asad narrated to us: al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us: Salih ibn Abi Jubayr narrated to us, from his father, from Rafi' ibn 'Amr, who said: I used to pelt the date-palms of the Ansar; they seized me and took me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: This one pelts our palms! He said: O Rafi', why do you pelt their palms? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am hungry! He said: Eat of what has fallen; may Allah fill you and give you drink."
"4463","'Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us: 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us. And Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-'Awfi narrated to us - both saying: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: After me there will be people of my ummah who recite the Qur'an and it does not pass their throats; they will exit the religion as the arrow exits the game, then never return to it - the worst of creation and creatures. Sulayman said: And most of my surmise is that he said: Their mark is shaving (the head). Ibn al-Samit said: I met Rafi' ibn 'Amr, the brother of al-Hakam ibn 'Amr al-Ghifari, and said to him: What of a hadith I heard from Abu Dharr, saying such-and-such - and I mentioned this hadith to him. He said: What amazes you of this? I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4464","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Abu Sa'id ibn al-Mu'alla - a man of his descendants named Rafi' ibn al-Mu'alla al-Ansari, of Banu Khubayb ibn 'Abd Harithah ibn Malik, narrated to me."
"4465","Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us: Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, concerning those martyred on the day of Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Muslims, of the Ansar, then of Banu Khubayb ibn 'Abd Harithah: Rafi' ibn al-Mu'alla."
"4466","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar: Rafi' ibn al-Mu'alla ibn Lawdhan ibn Harithah ibn 'Adi ibn Zayd ibn Tha'labah ibn Zayd ibn Manat ibn Khubayb ibn Harithah ibn Ghadb ibn Jusham ibn al-Khazraj - martyred on the day of Badr."
"4467","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Rafi' ibn al-Mu'alla ibn Lawdhan - martyred on the day of Badr."
"4468","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani narrated to us: 'Isam ibn 'Amr Abu Ahmad al-Ta'i narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Hayyan al-Ta'i narrated to us, who said: Rafi' ibn 'Umayrah al-Sinbisi used to feed the people of three mosques at midday and give them qurtumah to drink - meaning hays (a dish of dates, cheese and ghee) - though he had but one shirt: one for the house and one for Friday."
"4469","'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Ta'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent 'Amr ibn al-'As over the army of Dhat al-Salasil, and sent with him in that army Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and the elite of his companions. They set out until they camped at the mountain of Tayy, and 'Amr said: Look for a man who knows the road. They said: We know none but Rafi' ibn 'Amr, for he was a rabil in the Jahiliyyah - I asked Tariq: What is a rabil? He said: A thief who raids a people alone and steals. Rafi' said: When we had finished our campaign and I reached the place from which we had set out, I singled out Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and came to him, saying: O man of (noble) traits, I have singled you out from among your companions; give me something which, if I keep it, I shall be like you! He said: Do you keep (count of) your five fingers? I said: Yes. He said: Bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; establish the five prayers; give zakat if you have (wealth); perform Hajj to the House; fast Ramadan - have you kept it? I said: Yes. He said: And another: Never take command over two (men). I said: Is command ever but among you, the people of Badr? He said: It will soon spread until it reaches you and those below you. Allah - Mighty and Majestic - when He sent His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), people entered Islam: some entered and Allah guided them, and some the sword compelled, so he is Allah's oft-returning (servant), Allah's neighbour under Allah's protection. When a man is a commander and people wrong one another before him and he does not take for some of them from others, Allah takes vengeance upon him. A man's neighbour's sheep may be taken, and he remains with tensed muscle in anger for his neighbour, while Allah is behind the neighbour. Rafi' said: I stayed a year, then Abu Bakr was made caliph, so I rode to him and said: I am Rafi'; I met you on such-and-such a day at such-and-such a place! He said: I recognized (you). I said: You forbade me command, then you took upon yourself the greatest of it - the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! He said: Yes; whoever does not uphold among them the Book of Allah, upon him is the curse of Allah - meaning the curse of Allah."
"4470","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Rafi' ibn 'Amr al-Ta'i, who said: The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me on a campaign or Hajj, and I looked them over and saw none of them of finer appearance than Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), wrapped in a cloak against heat and cold; and he mentioned the hadith."
"4471","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Abi Mu'awiyah narrated to us: my father narrated to me, from al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Maysarah, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Rafi' ibn Abi Rafi' al-Ta'i, who said: In the campaign of Dhat al-Salasil, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed 'Amr ibn al-'As over an army containing Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4472","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar, then of Za'war ibn 'Abd al-Ashhal: Rafi' ibn Yazid."
"4473","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal: Rafi' ibn Sahl - also said to be Rafi' ibn Zayd."
"4474","Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'id: that a slave was owned among Banu Sa'id, and they freed him except one of them. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his intercession with the man, and he spoke to him concerning him, so the man gave his share to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed him. He used to say: I am the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); his name was Rafi', Abu al-Bahiyy."
"4475","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar: Rafi' ibn Ju'dubah."
"4476","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar: Rafi' ibn al-Harith ibn Sawwad ibn Zayd ibn Tha'labah."
"4477","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Najjar: Rafi' ibn al-Harith ibn Sawwad."
"4478","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Aws, then of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf, then of Banu Umayyah ibn Zayd: Rafi' ibn 'Unjudah."
"4479","Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah al-'Asqalani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us: my father narrated to me: Ibrahim ibn Abi 'Ablah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Rafi' ibn 'Umayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah - Mighty and Majestic - said to Dawud (peace be upon him): Build Me a house on earth! So Dawud built a house for himself before the House he was commanded with, and Allah - Mighty and Majestic - revealed to him: O Dawud, you have set up your own house before My house! He said: O Lord, thus You have decreed as You ordained: whoever owns takes preference. Then he began building the mosque (Temple); when the wall was completed, two-thirds of it collapsed, and he complained of that to Allah - Mighty and Majestic. Allah - Mighty and Majestic - revealed to him that it is not fitting for you to build Me a house. He said: O Lord, why? He said: Because of the blood shed at your hands! He said: O Lord, was it not for Your love and Your sake? He said: Yes, but they are My servants and I am more merciful to them! That weighed heavily upon him, so Allah revealed to him: Do not grieve, for I shall decree its building at the hands of your son Sulayman. When Dawud died, Sulayman began its building; when it was completed, he offered sacrifices, slaughtered offerings, and gathered the Children of Israel. Allah - Mighty and Majestic - revealed to him: I have seen joy at the building of My house, so ask Me and I shall give you! He said: I ask You three traits: judgment coinciding with Your judgment, a kingdom befitting none after me, and that whoever comes to this House seeking only prayer in it leaves his sins as on the day his mother bore him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two of them he was given, and I hope he was given the third."
"4480","Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-Layth, who said: In the year forty-six, Ruwayfi' was appointed over Tripoli (Atrablus)."
"4481","Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf al-Misri narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us: Rabi'ah ibn Sulaym, the freedman of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan al-Tujibi, narrated to me, that he heard Hanash al-San'ani narrate from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit concerning the campaign of people toward the Maghrib, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the campaign of Khaybar: It has reached me that you trade the mithqal (weight) for half or two-thirds, and it is not proper except mithqal for mithqal and weight for weight."
"4482","'Abd al-Malik ibn Yahya ibn Bukayr al-Misri narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym, from Wafa' ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: O Allah, bless Muhammad and grant him the near station with You on the Day of Resurrection - my intercession becomes due for him."
"4483","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Ibn Hubayrah narrated to me, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym, from Wafa' ibn Shurayh, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: O Allah, bless Muhammad and grant him the near station with You on the Day of Resurrection - I shall intercede for him."
"4484","Musa ibn 'Isa ibn al-Mundhir al-Himsi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, the freedman of Tujib: Hanash al-San'ani narrated to me, who said: We campaigned in the Maghrib under Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari, and we conquered a village called Jarbah. Ruwayfi' stood among us as a preacher and said: I stand among you only with what I heard the Messenger of Allah stand among us with on the day of Hunayn when we conquered it. He said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not water his water to another's crop (i.e. have intercourse with a pregnant captive); whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not approach a non-virgin among the captives until he has established her freedom from pregnancy; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not sell booty until it is divided; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not ride a beast of the Muslims' fay' until, when he has worn it out, he returns it to it; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear a garment of the Muslims' fay' until, when he has worn it out, he returns it to it."
"4485","Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf al-Misri narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us: Rabi'ah ibn Abi Sulaym al-Tujibi narrated to me, that he heard Hanash al-San'ani narrate from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not ride a beast of the spoils until, when he has broken it down, he returns it to the spoils, nor (wear) a garment he wears until, when he has worn it out, he returns it to the spoils. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not water his water to another's child."
"4486","Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Abu Marzuq al-Tujibi, from Hanash al-San'ani, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4487","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ayyub narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Hanash, who said: I was present with Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit when he conquered Jarbah; when he had conquered it, he stood among us as a preacher and said: I say to you only what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us on the day of Hunayn. He said: It is not lawful for a man who believes in Allah and the Last Day to sell booty until it is divided; it is not lawful for a man who believes in Allah and the Last Day to ride a beast of the Muslims' fay' until, when he has worn it out, he returns it; nor to wear a garment until, when he has worn it out, he returns it to it."
"4488","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Bishr al-Kufi narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Hanash, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4489","Ubayd ibn Ghannam and al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Minqari, from al-Hasan: Thabit ibn Ruwayfi' of the people of Egypt - who used to command expeditions - narrated to me, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Beware of ghulul (stealing from booty), and of a man marrying a woman before the fay' is divided then returning her to the spoils, and wearing a garment until it wears out then returning it to the spoils."
"4490","Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us. And Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us - both saying: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: al-Harith ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Hanash al-San'ani, who said: We conquered a fortress with Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit among us in the campaign of Jarbah. Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit came upon us and said to us: Whoever of you has taken of these captives, let him not tread (have intercourse with) her until she menstruates, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is not lawful for a man to water his water to another's child."
"4491","Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, from Abu Marzuq al-Tujibi, from Hanash al-San'ani, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: It is not lawful for one who believes in Allah and the Last Day - or: whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not water his water to another's child."
"4492","Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Zakariyya al-Iyadi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from Ishaq, from Humayd ibn 'Abdullah al-'Adawi, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Hudhayfah, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah forbid treading (intercourse with) the pregnant woman until she gives birth, and he said: One of you increases his hearing and his sight; and (forbid) treading the captives until they are pure. Then he said: Beware of the usury of ghulul. We said: What is the usury of ghulul, O Messenger of Allah? He said: That one of you takes a garment and wears it until its substance is gone, then throws it into the spoils; and the beasts - he rides them until he wears them out, then brings them to the spoils."
"4493","Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah informed us: 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas al-Qitbani narrated to me, that Shuyaym ibn Bitan informed him, that he heard Shayban ibn Qays al-Qitbani say: Muhammad ibn Makhlad appointed Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit over the lower land. Shayban ibn Qays said: So we marched with him. He said: Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit informed me, who said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of us would take his brother's worn-out (camel) on condition of giving him half of what he gains as booty, and half for him - until one of us would get the arrowhead and feathers, and the other the shaft. Then Ruwayfi' said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ruwayfi', life may be long for you after me, so inform the people that whoever knots his beard, or wears a bow-string as a necklace (amulet), or cleans himself with the dung of his beast or with a bone - Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is free of him."
"4494","Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir and Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, both saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah: Suhaym informed him, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, who said: Dates, fresh and dry, were brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they ate of them until nothing remained but stones and what is of no good. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know what this is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best! He said: The good goes away, then the good, until none remains of you but like this."
"4495","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, who said: I heard Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit say: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The taker of the maks (unjust tolls/taxes) is in the Fire - meaning the 'ashir (tithe-collector)."
"4496","Rifaa ibn Abd al-Mundhir, Abu Lubaba al-Ansari, of Banu Amr ibn Awf then of Banu Umayya ibn Zayd; also said to be Bishr ibn Abd al-Mundhir, and also said to be Bashir ibn Abd al-Mundhir. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa: Bashir ibn Abd al-Mundhir ibn al-Zinbir and al-Harith ibn Hatib went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Badr, and he sent them back, put Abu Lubaba in charge of Medina, and struck for them two shares along with the people of Badr."
"4497","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Amr ibn Awf, then of Banu Umayya ibn Zayd: Abu Lubaba Bashir ibn Abd al-Mundhir."
"4498","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr say: Abu Lubaba is Rifaa ibn Abd al-Mundhir."
"4499","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abd Allah from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir ibn Zinbir ibn Umayya ibn Zayd ibn Malik ibn Awf ibn Amr ibn Awf ibn Malik ibn al-Aws; he had gone out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Badr, but he sent him back and put him in charge of Medina, struck for him his share, and counted him along with the people of Badr."
"4500","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Kill the snakes; kill the one with two stripes and the tail-less one, for they cause miscarriage and blind the eyesight. Ibn Umar said: Abu Lubaba or Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw me chasing a snake to kill it and forbade me, so I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded killing them. He said: He later forbade killing the house snakes. Al-Zuhri said: They are the awamir."
"4501","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Hatim ibn Ismail from Ibrahim ibn Mujammi from Ibn Shihab from Salim from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill the snakes. Ibn Umar said: Abu Lubaba and Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw me killing a house snake, which we used to call jinnan, and they said: O Abdullah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing house snakes."
"4502","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Malik from Nafi from Abu Lubaba: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan that are in houses, except the one with two stripes and the tail-less one, for they snatch the eyesight and cause women to miscarry what is in their wombs."
"4503","Narrated al-Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Abu Lubaba: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan that are in houses."
"4504","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma from Juwayriya ibn Asma from Nafi from Ibn Umar: Abu Lubaba informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan found in houses."
"4505","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abd Allah ibn Numayr from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Abu Lubaba, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan that are in houses."
"4506","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Abu Lubaba, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan found in houses."
"4507","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Yahya from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar: Nafi narrated to me that he heard Abu Lubaba inform Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan."
"4508","Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Abu Musab from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Dinar from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar: Abu Lubaba informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan that are in houses, and said: Each of you is responsible for his flock; the ruler over the people is a shepherd responsible for his flock, a man is a shepherd over his household responsible for them, a man's wife is a shepherdess over her husband's house responsible for it, and a man's slave is in charge of his master's wealth responsible for it."
"4509","Narrated Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni from Muhammad ibn Rumh from Ibn Lahi'a from Yazid ibn Abi Habib: Bukayr ibn Abd Allah informed him from Nafi from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that after Abu Lubaba informed him of what he reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever he saw one he threatened it and advanced toward it, and sometimes he would order us together to carry it until we cast it out."
"4510","Narrated Abbas ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Aqil al-Baghdadi from Mahmud ibn Ghaylan from Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani from Hisham ibn Hassan from Ayyub from Nafi: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) used to kill all the jinnan found in houses, until Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the jinnan that are in houses, so he left them alone thereafter, though he would kill anything besides them."
"4511","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Nuaym ibn Hammad from Ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsa from al-Zuhri from al-Husayn ibn al-Saib ibn Abi Lubaba from his father, who said: When Allah accepted the repentance of Abu Lubaba, Abu Lubaba said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I shall abandon the dwelling of my people where I committed the sin, and divest myself of my wealth as charity for Allah and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Lubaba, a third will suffice from you. He said: So I gave a third in charity."
"4512","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal from Yaqub ibn Humayd from Abd Allah ibn Musa al-Tamimi from Usama ibn Zayd from Ibn Shihab: One of the sons of al-Saib ibn Abi Lubaba narrated to me from Abu Lubaba that he said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I abandon the dwelling of my people where I committed the sin, and divest myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A third of your wealth will suffice from you."
"4513","Narrated Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi from Abu Hudhayfa from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad; and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba; and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Ali ibn Bahr and Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, who said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us from Zuhayr from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jariya from Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Friday is the chief of days and the greatest of them before Allah, greater than the day of sacrifice and the day of breaking the fast; in it are five traits: Allah created Adam in it, Allah sent Adam down to earth in it, Allah took Adam's soul in it, in it there is an hour in which a servant does not ask Allah for anything but He gives it to him so long as he does not ask for what is unlawful, and in it the Hour will come; there is no close angel, nor heaven, nor earth, nor sea, but that it dreads Friday."
"4514","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughira from Amr ibn Thabit from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jariya from Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Friday is the chief of days - then he mentioned the like of it."
"4515","Narrated Ahmad ibn Ma Bahram al-Idhaji from Ishaq ibn Ziyad al-Qattan from Yaqub ibn Muhammad from Asim ibn Suwayd from Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Husayn ibn al-Saib ibn Abi Lubaba, who said: My father narrated to me from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Badr: How will you fight the enemy when you meet them? Asim ibn Thabit stood up and said: O Messenger of Allah, when the enemy are at a distance our arrows can reach, we shoot arrows; when they come close enough for stones to reach us and them, we pelt with stones, taking three stones in hand and two stones in the girdle-pouch; when they come close enough for spears to reach us and them, we thrust with spears; and when the spears break, we strike with swords. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is how war was ordained; whoever fights, let him fight as Asim fights."
"4516","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi from Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ward, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka say: I heard Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Nahik say: While I was standing with Abd Allah ibn al-Saib ibn Abi al-Saib, Abu Lubaba passed by us, so we followed him until he entered his house; we asked permission and he admitted us, and there he was with worn furnishings and a worn state. He said: Who are you? So we stated our lineage to him, and he said: Welcome, earning merchants. Then I heard him say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran melodiously. I said to Ibn Abi Mulayka: O Abu Muhammad, what if one does not have a beautiful voice? He said: He beautifies it as much as he can."
"4517","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Uqba ibn Mukram from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The two men who lived farthest from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were two men of the Ansar, Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir, whose dwelling was at Quba, and Abu Yahya among Banu Haritha; they would pray Asr with him then come to their people who had not yet prayed, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr early."
"4518","Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Zuraqi al-Ansari, Aqabi and Badri. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of the people of al-Aqaba among the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Rifaa ibn Rafi ibn Malik ibn Ajlan ibn Amr ibn Zurayq, a naqib, and he attended Badr."
"4519","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Rifaa ibn Rafi ibn Malik ibn Ajlan."
"4520","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd Allah al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abd Allah from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, then of al-Khazraj, then of Banu Zurayq: Rifaa ibn Rafi ibn Malik ibn al-Ajlan ibn Amr ibn Asim ibn Zurayq ibn Abd Haritha ibn Malik ibn Ghadb ibn Jusham ibn al-Khazraj."
"4521","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Dirar ibn Surad from Ali ibn Hashim from Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi from his father, regarding the naming of those who were with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Rifaa ibn Rafi, a Badri, of Banu Zurayq."
"4522","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Dawud ibn Qays from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi ibn Malik al-Zuraqi: My father narrated to me from his uncle, who was a Badri, who said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, a man entered and prayed two rakas while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him; then he came and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he returned the greeting, then said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, then came and greeted him, and he returned the greeting, then said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He repeated it a third or fourth time, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying his first words. He said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, by the One who sent down the Book to you, I have striven and been keen, so show me and teach me. He said: When you intend to pray, perform wudu and perfect your wudu, then face the qibla and say the takbir and recite, then bow until you are tranquil while bowing, then rise until you stand upright, then prostrate until you are tranquil while prostrating, then rise until you sit tranquil, then prostrate until you are tranquil while prostrating, then rise; if you complete your prayer in this way you have completed it, and whatever you omit of this you omit from yourself."
"4523","Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Muhammad ibn Ajlan from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad al-Zuraqi from his father from his uncle, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque when a man was praying in a corner of the mosque, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him without him noticing. When he finished praying he came forward, stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it, then said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, then came and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he returned it, then said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He did that two or three times. The man said: By the One who sent down the Book to you in truth, I have striven and been keen, so show me, O Messenger of Allah, and teach me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you intend to pray, perform wudu and perfect your wudu as Allah commanded, then recite, then bow until you are tranquil while bowing, then rise until you stand straight, then prostrate until you are tranquil while prostrating, then rise until you sit level, then prostrate until you are tranquil while prostrating, then stand; if you complete your prayer in this way it is complete, and if you fall short of that, you fall short in your prayer."
"4524","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Muhammad ibn Ajlan from Ali ibn Yahya of the family of Rifaa ibn Rafi from his father from a paternal uncle of his, a Badri, who narrated to him: A man entered the mosque and prayed while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him without him knowing; when he finished he came forward and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to him: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to him: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, two or three times. The man said: By the One who honored you, O Messenger of Allah, I have striven, so teach me. He said to him: When you stand intending prayer, perform wudu and perfect your wudu, then face the qibla, then recite, then bow and be tranquil, then prostrate and be tranquil while prostrating, then rise until you sit tranquil, then prostrate until you are tranquil while prostrating, then rise, then do that until you finish your prayer."
"4525","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Yahya from Ibn Ajlan from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from his father from his uncle, who was a Badri, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque when a man entered and prayed - then he mentioned the like of it."
"4526","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Khalid al-Ahmar from Ibn Ajlan from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from his father from his uncle, who was a Badri, who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came praying a light prayer, not completing its bowing or prostration, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"4527","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal; and narrated Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini from Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, who said: Hammam narrated to us from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talha from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from his father from his uncle Rifaa ibn Rafi - Abu al-Walid added in his narration: Rifaa and Malik were two brothers among the people of Badr - who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting he looked around, and there was a man who faced the qibla and prayed two rakas - and Hajjaj said in his narration: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came and entered the mosque - when he finished his prayer he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And upon you, go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, and he kept watching his prayer, not knowing what fault was in it; when he finished his prayer he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted the people, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He mentioned either two or three times. The man said: I do not know what fault you found in me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of any of you is not complete until he performs wudu thoroughly as Allah commanded him: he washes his face and his hands to the elbows, wipes his head and his feet to the ankles, then declares Allah great, praises Him, and recites of the Quran what Allah has permitted and made easy for him, then says the takbir and bows, placing his palms on his knees until his joints are settled and relaxed, then says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, and stands upright until every bone takes its place and he straightens his spine, then says the takbir and prostrates, placing his forehead - Hammam said: and perhaps he said his face - firmly on the ground until his joints are settled and relaxed, then says the takbir and raises his head and sits level on his seat and straightens his spine. So he described the prayer thus until he finished, then said: The prayer of any of you is not complete until he does that; the wording is that of Hajjaj's narration."
"4528","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj from Hammad from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talha from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from his uncle: A man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting, and prayed; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him, and he repeated two or three times. He said: O Messenger of Allah, after two or three times I did my utmost to complete my prayer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer of anyone among the people is complete until he performs wudu and puts the wudu in its places, then says: Allah is greatest, then praises Allah and extols Him and recites what is easy of the Quran, then says the takbir until his joints are settled, then says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, until he stands straight, then says the takbir and prostrates until his joints are settled, then raises his head until he sits straight, then prostrates until his joints are settled; if he does not do that, his prayer is not complete."
"4529","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Rishdin al-Misri from his father from his grandfather from Amr ibn al-Harith from Said ibn Abi Hilal from Yahya ibn Ali ibn Yahya from his father from his grandfather from Rifaa ibn Rafi: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying in the mosque and said: Go back and pray, saying it two or three times; then he said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me. He said: When you stand for prayer, perform wudu as Allah commanded you, then stand and face the qibla and say the takbir; if you have Quran with you, recite, otherwise glorify Allah and declare Him great; then bow and place your palms firmly on your knees, then rise until your spine is straight, then prostrate and place your forehead firmly on the ground, then do that; when you do that your prayer is complete, and whatever you omit of that you have omitted from your prayer."
"4530","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Ismail ibn Ibrahim; and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Ismail ibn Ulaya from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi al-Ansari from his father from his uncle Rifaa ibn Rafi, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of the Ansar came after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had finished the prayer, and prayed; then he came forward and greeted him, and he said: And upon you peace, go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have striven, so make it clear to me. He said: When you stand in your prayer, declare Allah great, then recite what is easy for you of the Quran; then when you bow, fix your hands on your knees until every bone of yours is settled; then when you raise your head, stand straight until every bone of yours returns; then when you prostrate, be tranquil until every bone of yours is level; then when you raise your head, remain firm until every bone returns to its place; then likewise; and when you sit in the middle of your prayer, be tranquil, spread your left thigh, then recite the tashahhud; then when you stand, do likewise until you finish your prayer."
"4531","Narrated Bakr ibn Muhammad al-Qazzaz al-Basri from Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi from Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi from Muhammad ibn Amr from Ali ibn Yahya from Rifaa ibn Rafi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"4532","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad; and narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Ubayd Allah ibn Sad from his uncle from his father from Sharik from Abd Allah ibn Awn from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from Rifaa ibn Rafi, who was a Badri, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was sitting in the mosque, prayed near him, then left and stood and greeted him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Repeat your prayer, for you have not prayed. He prayed roughly as he had prayed, then left and stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Repeat your prayer, for you have not prayed. He said: O Prophet of Allah, teach me. He said: When you face the qibla, say the takbir, then recite whatever Allah wills you to recite; when you bow, place your palms on your knees, extend your back, and make your bowing firm; when you rise, straighten your spine until the bones return to their joints; when you prostrate, make your prostration firm; when you rise, sit on your left thigh; and do likewise in every raka and prostration."
"4533","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi; and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf; and narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Ismail ibn Abi Uways - all from Malik from Nuaym al-Mujmir from Ali ibn Yahya al-Zuraqi from his father from Rifaa ibn Rafi, who said: One day we were praying behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he raised his head from the bowing and said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, a man behind him said: Our Lord, and to You is praise, abundant, good, blessed praise. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said: Who was speaking just now? The man said: I was, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw more than thirty angels racing to it, which of them would write it first."
"4534","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Qutayba ibn Said; and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Said ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Karabisi, who said: Rifaa ibn Yahya, imam of the mosque of Banu Zurayq in Medina, narrated to us, who said: I heard Muadh ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi from his father Rifaa: He prayed Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Rifaa sneezed and said: Praise be to Allah, abundant, good, blessed praise, blessed upon it, as our Lord loves and is pleased. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished praying, he said: Where is the one who spoke during the prayer? Rifaa said: I wished I had paid a fine from my wealth rather than having attended that prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he said: Where is the speaker? So I said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said: What did you say? He said: I said: Praise be to Allah, abundant, good, blessed praise, blessed upon it, as our Lord loves and is pleased. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, more than thirty angels raced to it, which of them would ascend with it."
"4535","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Ibrahim ibn Yahya al-Shajari from his father from Ubayd ibn Yahya from Muadh ibn Rifaa from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, and the sons of the Ansar, and the sons of the sons of the Ansar, and their offspring and their neighbors."
"4536","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Arara; and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, who said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us from Hisham ibn Harun al-Madani al-Ansari from Muadh ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Ansari from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, and the sons of the Ansar, and their offspring and their neighbors."
"4537","Narrated Masada ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran from Rifaa ibn Yahya from Muadh ibn Rifaa from Rafi, who said: On the day of Badr the people crowded upon Umayya ibn Khalaf; I came forward and saw a piece of his armor cut open beneath his armpit, so I stabbed him with the sword and killed him; and I was shot with an arrow on the day of Badr and my eye was gouged, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat in it and supplicated for me, and nothing of it ever hurt me."
"4538","Narrated al-Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Mamar ibn Abi Habiba from Ubayd ibn Rifaa: Zayd ibn Thabit used to preach, and said in his preaching: If a man is intimate with a woman and does not ejaculate, no ghusl is due upon him; let him wash his private part and perform wudu. A man in the gathering stood up and mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bring him to me so I may be a witness against him. When he came to him he said to him: O enemy of yourself, you misguide people without knowledge. He said: O Commander of the Believers, by Allah I did not innovate it, but I heard it from my paternal uncles. He said: Which of your uncles? He said: Ubayy ibn Kab, Rifaa ibn Rafi, and Abu Ayyub. Rifaa, who was present, said: Do not scold him, O Commander of the Believers, for by Allah we used to do this in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Did you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) learned of any of that? He said: No. Ali ibn Abi Talib said: O Commander of the Believers, this matter is not sound. He said: Whom shall I ask after you, O best people of Badr? Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Send to the Mothers of the Believers. So he sent to Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: I have no knowledge. So he sent to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl is obligatory. Then they delved into mentioning coitus interruptus and said: No harm. Then a man whispered to his companion and said: What is this whispering? One of them claims it is the minor female burial. Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: It is not a buried infant until it passes through seven stages; Allah Almighty said: We created man from an extract of clay, then We made him a drop in a firm lodging, then We made the drop a clot, then We made the clot a lump, then We made the lump bones, then We clothed the bones with flesh, then We produced him as another creation; so blessed is Allah, the best of creators. So they dispersed upon the word of Ali ibn Abi Talib that there is no harm in it."
"4539","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Abd Allah ibn Idris and Abd al-Ala from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Mamar ibn Abi Habiba from Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father, who said: We used to do it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when we did not ejaculate we did not perform ghusl."
"4540","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Asad ibn Musa from Ibn Lahi'a from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj from Khallad ibn al-Saib from Rifaa al-Ansari: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No fewer than twenty verses are recited in Fajr, and no fewer than ten verses are recited in Isha."
"4541","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Abu Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father from his grandfather, who said: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the market, and he said: O company of merchants. The merchants raised their eyes to him and responded to him, and he said: The merchants will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as wicked, except one who feared Allah, was dutiful, and truthful."
"4542","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Sufyan from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to al-Baqi and said: O company of merchants, until they craned their necks. He said: The merchants will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection as wicked, except one who feared Allah, was dutiful, and truthful."
"4543","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal and Ismail ibn Ibrahim, who said: Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Zuraqi from his father from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in Medina and found people trading, so he called: O company of merchants. They responded to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and raised their eyes to him, and he said: The merchants will be raised as wicked, except one who feared Allah, was dutiful, and truthful."
"4544","Narrated Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari from al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi al-Kufi; and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi; and narrated Abu Habib Yahya ibn Nafi al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam, who said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa al-Ansari then al-Zuraqi from his father from his grandfather: He went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the prayer ground in Medina and found people trading, so he said: O company of merchants. They responded and raised their necks and eyes to him, and he said: The merchants will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as wicked, except one who feared Allah, was truthful, and dutiful."
"4545","Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal from Ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"4546","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Zuhayr from Abd Allah ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi from his father Ubayd from Rifaa ibn Rafi: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): Gather your people for me, O Umar. So he gathered them, and when they arrived at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Umar entered to him and said: I have gathered your people for you. The Ansar heard that and said: Revelation has come down concerning Quraysh, so the listener and the watcher came to see what he would say to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and stood among them and said: Is there among you any who is not of you? They said: Yes, among us are our ally, our sister's son, and our freedman. The Messenger of Allah said: Our ally is of us, our sister's son is of us, and our freedman is of us. You are listening: my allies on the Day of Resurrection are the God-fearing; if you are those, then that is it, otherwise beware: let not people come with deeds on the Day of Resurrection while you come with burdens and are turned away from. Then he called out and said: O people, Quraysh are people of trust; whoever seeks against them calamities or stumbles - Zuhayr said: and I think it is stumbles - Allah will throw him on his nostril. He said that three times."
"4547","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father from his grandfather: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Gather your people. So he gathered them at the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then entered to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I admit them to you or will you come out to them? He said: No, rather I shall come out to them. He came to them and stood over them and said: Is there among you any who is not of you? They said: Yes; among us are our allies, among us are our brothers' sons, and among us are our freedmen. He said: Our ally is of us, and our brother's son is of us. He said: You are listening: my allies among you are the God-fearing; if you are those, then that is it, otherwise beware: let not people come with deeds on the Day of Resurrection while you come with burdens and I turn away from you. Then he raised his hands while standing and they were sitting and said: O people, Quraysh are people of trust, and whoever seeks stumbles for them, Allah will throw him on his nostril. He said it three times."
"4548","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Zuraqi from his father from his grandfather: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar: Gather your people - then he mentioned the like of it."
"4549","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Waki from Sufyan from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa from his father from his grandfather: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar: Gather your people for me. He gathered them and they were at the door, and he said: Is there among you anyone who is not of you? They said: No, only our sister's son and our freedman. He said: Your sister's son and your freedman are of you. He said: My allies among you are the God-fearing; beware lest people come to me with deeds while you come to me with burdens carried on your backs. Then he said: Quraysh are people of patience and trust; whoever seeks stumbles for them, Allah will throw him on his face on the Day of Resurrection."
"4550","Narrated Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi from Asim ibn Ali Abu Mashar from Ibrahim ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi ibn Malik ibn Ajlan al-Ansari from his father from his grandfather, who said: We advanced on the day of Badr and missed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the companies called out to one another: Is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among you? They halted until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, with him Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him). They said: O Messenger of Allah, we missed you. He said: Abu Hasan felt colic in his stomach, so I stayed back with him."
"4551","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father; and narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Dawud ibn Umar al-Dabbi; and narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman, who said: Marwan ibn Muawiya narrated to us from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman from Ubayd ibn Rifaa al-Zuraqi from his father, who said: On the day of Uhud, when the polytheists turned back, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand straight so I may praise my Lord. They formed ranks behind him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, to You is all praise; none withholds what You extend, none extends what You withhold, none guides whom You lead astray, none leads astray whom You guide, none brings near what You distance, none distances what You bring near, none gives what You deny, none denies what You give. O Allah, spread upon us of Your blessings, mercy, bounty, and provision. O Allah, I ask You for lasting bliss on the day of need and security on the day of fear. O Allah, I seek one who takes refuge in You from the evil of what You gave us and the evil of what You withheld from us. O Allah, take us as Muslims and join us with the righteous, neither disgraced nor afflicted. O Allah, fight the disbelievers who bar from Your path and deny Your messengers. O Allah, fight the disbelievers who were given the Book, O God of truth."
"4552","Narrated Masada ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn Mundhir al-Hizami from Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to me from Abd Rabbih ibn Said ibn Qays al-Ansari from Rifaa ibn Rafi, who said: When Iblis saw what the angels were doing to the polytheists on the day of Badr, he feared the killing would reach him, so al-Harith ibn Hisham clung to him, thinking he was Suraqa ibn Malik; he struck al-Harith in the chest and threw him down, then fled until he cast himself into the sea and raised his hands and said: I ask You for Your respite to me. And he feared the killing would reach him. Then Abu Jahl ibn Hisham came forward and said: O people, let not the desertion of Suraqa dishearten you, for he was on an appointment with Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and let not the killing of Utba, Shayba, and al-Walid dismay you, for they hastened; by al-Lat and al-Uzza we shall not return until we bind them with ropes, and let no man among you kill any of them, but take them captive so you make them taste the evil of their deed in abandoning you and turning from al-Lat and al-Uzza. Then Abu Jahl said, versifying: ... [verse truncated in source: sinni ...]"
"4553","Rifaa ibn Amr ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Thalaba ibn Malik ibn Salim, Aqabi and Badri, martyred on the day of Uhud. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of the people of al-Aqaba: Rifaa ibn Amr ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Qays ibn Thalaba ibn Malik ibn Salim ibn Ghanim ibn Awf ibn al-Khazraj; he attended Badr, and he was among those who went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as emigrants."
"4554","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqaba, then of Banu al-Hubla: Rifaa ibn Amr; and he attended Badr."
"4555","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Uhud, then of Banu Sawa: Rifaa ibn Amr."
"4556","Rifaa ibn Abd al-Mundhir ibn Rifaa ibn Dinar al-Ansari, Aqabi and Badri. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those who attended al-Aqaba among the Ansar, then of Banu Zafar - and Zafar is Kab ibn al-Khazraj: Rifaa ibn al-Mundhir ibn Rifaa ibn Dinar ibn Zayd ibn Umayya ibn Malik ibn Awf ibn Amr ibn Awf; he attended Badr."
"4557","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Amr ibn Awf, then of Banu Umayya ibn Zayd: Rifaa ibn Abd al-Mundhir."
"4558","Rifaa ibn Araba al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn al-Muhajir al-Raqqi from Muhammad ibn Musab al-Qarqasai; and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Amr ibn Hashim al-Bayruti; and narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Bablutti, who said: Al-Awzai narrated to us from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Hilal ibn Abi Maymuna from Ata ibn Yasar from Rifaa ibn Araba, who said: We returned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and people began asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for leave, and he gave them leave. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is it with you that the side of the tree nearer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more hateful to you than the other side? None of the people was to be seen but weeping. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One who asks your leave after this is, in my view, a fool. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, and said: I testify before Allah - and when he swore he said: By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad - there is none of you who believes in Allah and then stays upright but that he is led into Paradise; my Lord Almighty has promised me to admit to Paradise seventy thousand of my umma with no reckoning and no punishment, and I hope they will not enter it until you and those who are righteous of your wives and offspring take dwellings in Paradise. Then he said: When half the night has passed - or he said: two thirds - Allah Almighty descends to the lowest heaven and says: I ask none about My servants but Me; who is it that asks Me, that I may give him? Who is it that calls upon Me, that I may answer him? Who is it that seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Until dawn breaks."
"4559","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Kaysan al-Massisi from Habban ibn Hilal; and narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz from Musa ibn Ismail, who said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Hilal ibn Abi Maymuna from Ata ibn Yasar: Rifaa ibn Araba al-Juhani narrated to him, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and people began asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for leave, and he gave them leave. He said: What is it with you that the side of the tree nearer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more hateful to you than the other side? None of the people was to be seen but weeping. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One who asks your leave after this is, in my view, a fool. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, and said: I testify before Allah - and when he swore he said: By the One in whose hand is my soul - there is none of you who believes in Allah and then stays upright but that he is led into Paradise; my Lord has promised me to admit to Paradise seventy thousand of my umma with no reckoning and no punishment, and I hope they will not enter it until you and those who are righteous of your wives and offspring take dwellings in Paradise. Then he said: When half the night has passed - or he said: a third of the night - Allah Almighty descends to the lowest heaven and says: Who is it that seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Who is it that calls upon Me, that I may answer him? Who is it that asks Me, that I may give him? Until dawn breaks."
"4560","Narrated Muhammad ibn Muadh al-Halabi from Abd Allah ibn Raja from Harb ibn Shaddad from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Hilal ibn Abi Maymuna: Ata ibn Yasar narrated to him that Rifaa al-Juhani narrated to him, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until when we were at al-Kadid, they began asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for leave to go to their families, and he gave them leave. He said: What is it with you that the side of the tree nearer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more hateful to you than the other side? After that none of the people was to be seen but weeping. A man of the people said: O Messenger of Allah, one who asks your leave after this is a fool. So he praised Allah and extolled Him well, then said: I testify before Allah: no servant dies bearing witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, sincerely from his heart, then stays upright, but that he is led into Paradise; my Lord has promised me to admit to Paradise seventy thousand of my umma with no reckoning and no punishment, and I hope you will not enter until you and those who are righteous of your wives and offspring take dwellings in Paradise. And he said: When half or a third of the night has passed, Allah Almighty descends to the lowest heaven and says: I ask none about My servants but Me; who is it that seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Who is it that calls upon Me, that I may answer him? Who is it that asks Me, that I may give him? Until dawn breaks."
"4561","Narrated Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from Muadh ibn Hisham from his father from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Hilal ibn Abi Maymuna from Ata ibn Yasar from Rifaa ibn Araba - Abu Musa said: thus said Muadh ibn Hisham, and he is Ibn Araba - that he narrated to him, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until when we were at al-Kadid - or he said at Qudayd - men among us began asking leave to go to their families, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them leave. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"4562","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Shayban ibn Farrukh from Abu Umayya al-Habati from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Hilal ibn Abi Maymuna from Ata ibn Yasar from Rifaa ibn Araba, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until when we were at al-Kadid. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"4563","Rifaa ibn Aws al-Ansari, martyred on the day of Uhud. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Uhud: Rifaa ibn Aws ibn Zawr ibn Abd al-Ashhal."
"4564","Rifaa ibn Zayd al-Judhami. Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Rifaa ibn Zayd al-Judhami came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the truce of Hudaybiyya, gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a slave boy as a gift, and embraced Islam, and his Islam was good; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for him to his people a letter in it: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; this is a letter from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Rifaa ibn Zayd; I have sent him to his people generally and whoever joins them, calling them to Allah and to His Messenger; whoever comes forward is in the party of Allah and His Messenger, and whoever turns back has protection for two months. When Rifaa came to his people they responded and embraced Islam."
"4565","Rifaa ibn Qaraza al-Qurazi. Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami from Hammad ibn Salama from Amr ibn Dinar from Yahya ibn Jada from Rifaa al-Qurazi, who said: This verse was revealed concerning ten men, of whom I am one: And We have conveyed the word to them that they might remember."
"4566","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from al-Aswad ibn Amir Shadhan from Hammad ibn Salama from Amr ibn Dinar from Yahya ibn Jada: Rifaa ibn Qaraza said: This verse was revealed concerning ten, of whom I am one: And We have conveyed the word - to the end of the verse."
"4567","Rifaa ibn Samaw'al al-Qurazi. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Malik ibn Anas from al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zubayr from his father. [Isnad ends here in source; matn not preserved in this row.]"
"4568","Narrated Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Yunus from Ibn Shihab from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal: Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabia ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib informed him that his father Rabia ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib and Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said to Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabia and al-Fadl ibn Abbas: Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Messenger of Allah, we have reached the age you see; we wish to marry, and you, O Messenger of Allah, are the kindest and most joining of people, yet our fathers have nothing to pay dowries on our behalf, so employ us, O Messenger of Allah, over the charities; we shall deliver to you what the workers deliver, and take whatever benefit is in them. Ali ibn Abi Talib came to us while we were in that state and said to us: No, by Allah, he will not employ any of you over charity. Rabia ibn al-Harith said to him: This is from your envy and hostility; you attained kinship by marriage with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we did not envy you for it. So Ali cast down his cloak and lay upon it, then said: I am Abu al-Hasan today; by Allah I shall not leave this place until your two sons return to you with the answer to what you sent them with to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abd al-Muttalib said: I and al-Fadl set out until we arrived as the Zuhr prayer was established; we prayed with the people, then I and al-Fadl hastened to the door of the chamber of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he was that day with Zaynab bint Jahsh - and we stood at the door until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, took hold of my ear and al-Fadl's ear, and said: Tell me what you are whispering. Then he entered and gave me and al-Fadl leave, and we entered; we left the speaking to each other a little, then I spoke to him, or al-Fadl spoke to him - Abd Allah doubted that - and we spoke to him what our fathers had commanded us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent, then raised his gaze toward the ceiling of the house, until it was long upon us that he would return nothing to us, until we saw Zaynab signalling from behind the curtain with her hand that we should not be hasty, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was considering our matter. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lowered his head and said to us: This charity is but the filth of people; it is not lawful for Muhammad nor for the family of Muhammad. Call Nawfal ibn al-Harith for me. Nawfal ibn al-Harith was called for him, and he said: O Nawfal, marry Abd al-Muttalib. So Nawfal married me. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call Mahmiya ibn Jaz for me - a man of Banu Zubayd whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had employed over the fifths - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Mahmiya: Marry al-Fadl. So he married him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand and pay dowries on their behalf from the fifth, such-and-such. Abd Allah ibn al-Harith did not hear it."
"4569","Narrated Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli from Ali ibn al-Madini from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim from his father from Salih ibn Kaysan from Ibn Shihab from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith: Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabia informed him, who said: Rabia ibn al-Harith and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib gathered - then he mentioned the like of the lengthy narration of Yunus."
"4570","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Azhar ibn Marwan al-Raqashi from Abd al-Ala from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from al-Zuhri from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Nawfal from Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabia, who said: Rabia ibn al-Harith and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib gathered - then he mentioned the like of the narration of Yunus. And al-Zuhri narrated this narration from three brothers: from Abd Allah, Ubayd Allah, and Muhammad, sons of Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal."
"4571","Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, kunya Abu Firas. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman from Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, who said: I used to sleep in the chamber of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would hear him when he rose at night praying, saying: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, for a long while, then saying: Glory be to my Lord, the Almighty, and with His praise, for a long while. I said: What is al-hawiyy? He said: He supplicates for a while."
"4572","Narrated Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani from Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Bablutti from al-Awzai from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama from Rabia ibn Kab, who said: I used to spend the night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), bringing him his wudu water and his needs; he would rise at night saying: Glory be to my Lord and with His praise, glory be to my Lord and with His praise, for a long while; Glory be to the Lord of the worlds, glory be to the Lord of the worlds, for a long while. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have a need? I said: O Messenger of Allah, your companionship in Paradise. He said: Then help me for yourself with much prostration."
"4573","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Hajjaj ibn Nusayr from Hisham al-Dastuwai from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama from Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, who said: I used to spend the night at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would hear him for a long while of the night saying: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, and I would hear him for a long while of the night saying: Glory be to my Lord and with His praise."
"4574","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Hajjaj ibn Nusayr from Ali ibn al-Mubarak from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama from Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"4575","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abda al-Massisi from Abu Tawba al-Rabi ibn Nafi; and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn Bishr al-Hariri, who said: Muawiya ibn Sallam narrated to us from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama: Rabia ibn Kab informed him that he used to spend the night at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say at night: Glory be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, for a long while, then say: Glory be to my Lord and with His praise, and the like of that."
"4576","Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad from Adam from Shayban from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama from Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, who said: I used to spend the night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would hear him for a long while of the night saying: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, then saying: Glory be to my Lord and with His praise, for a long while."
"4577","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Abd al-Warith from Husayn al-Muallim from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him that he heard Rabia ibn Kab say: I spent the night at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I heard him say when he rose at night: Glory be to the Lord of the worlds, for a long while, then say: Glory be to my Lord and with His praise, for a long while."
"4578","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata from Nuaym al-Mujmir from Rabia ibn Kab, who said: I used to serve the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by day, and when night came I would resort to the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spend the night with him; I kept hearing him say: Glory be to Allah, glory be to my Lord, until I grew weary or my eyes overcame me and I slept. One day he said: O Rabia, ask me and I shall give you. I said: Give me time to consider. I remembered that this world is fleeting and cut off, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I ask you to supplicate Allah to keep me from the Fire and admit me to Paradise. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent, then said: Who told you to say this? I said: No one told me; rather I knew this world is cut off and fleeting, and you are in the station with Allah that you are in, so I wished you would supplicate Allah. He said: I shall do so; then help me with much prostration."
"4579","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Asad ibn Musa; and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Amr ibn Marzuq, who said: Mubarak ibn Fadala narrated to us from Abu Imran al-Jawni from Rabia al-Aslami, who said: I used to serve the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he gave me land and gave Abu Bakr land, and worldly goods came, and we differed over a cluster of a palm tree; Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: It is within the boundary of my land, while I said: It is within my boundary. There were words between me and Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr said to me a word I disliked, and he regretted it, and said to me: O Rabia, return the like of it to me so there is requital. I said: I shall not do it. Abu Bakr said: You shall say it, or I shall seek the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against you. I said: I am not one to do it. He abandoned the land, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) set out to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I set out following him; some people of Aslam came and said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Bakr; for what does he seek the Messenger of Allah against you, when he is the one who said to you what he said? I said: Do you know who this is? This is Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, the second of the two, the elder of the Muslims; beware, lest he turn and see you aiding me against him and grow angry, then come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who grows angry for his anger, then Allah grows angry for their anger, and Rabia perishes. They said: What do you command us? He said: Go back. So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) set out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I followed him alone, trailing behind until he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him the story as it was. He raised his head to me and said: O Rabia, what is between you and al-Siddiq? I said: O Messenger of Allah, it was so-and-so; he said to me a word I disliked, and said to me: Say as I said to you so there is requital. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, but do not return it upon him; rather say: May Allah forgive you, O Abu Bakr, may Allah forgive you, O Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr turned away - may Allah have mercy on him - weeping."
"4580","Narrated Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Qadi from Amr ibn Marzuq from Mubarak ibn Fadala from Abu Imran al-Jawni from Rabia al-Aslami, who said: I used to serve the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one day he said: O Rabia, will you not marry? I said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, serving you is dearer to me. Then he repeated it to me after another time and I said the like of that. Then I said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knows better what is good for me than I do; if he says it to me another time I shall say: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he said to me: O Rabia, will you not marry? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said to me: Go to so-and-so - a man of the Ansar - and let them marry you to their daughter so-and-so. So I went to them and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you to marry me. They said: Welcome to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) goes only for his need. So they married me, without asking me for proof. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dejected, and he said to me: What is wrong with you, O Rabia? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I went to noble people who married me without asking me for proof, and I have nothing for a dowry. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gather for him the weight of a date-stone of gold. They gathered for me the weight of two date-stones of gold, and I brought it to them; they accepted and said: Ample and good. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dejected, and he said: What is wrong with you, O Rabia? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I went to noble people who accepted and said ample and good, but I have nothing for a wedding feast. He said: Gather for him the price of a ram. They gathered for me the price of a ram, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to his household and a basket of barley was brought; I brought it to them, and they said: As for the ram, you spare us it, and as for the barley, we shall spare you it. They did that, and in the morning I invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions."
"4581","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi from Muhammad ibn Umar al-Aslami from Abd Allah ibn Amir al-Aslami from Yahya ibn Bahz al-Aslami from Hanzala ibn Ali al-Aslami from Rabia ibn Kab al-Aslami, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe over his two leather socks."
"4582","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi from his father from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad al-Ammi from Abu Imran al-Jawni from Abu Firas, a man of Aslam, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Ask me whatever you wish. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, who is my father? He said: Your father is so-and-so to whom you are attributed. Another man asked him: Am I in Paradise? He said: In Paradise. Another said: Am I in Paradise? He said: In the Fire. Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up and said: We are pleased with Allah as Lord."
"4583","Rabia ibn Qays al-Adwani. Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Dirar ibn Surad from Ali ibn Hashim from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Rafi from his father, regarding the naming of those who were with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Rabia ibn Qays, of Adwan."
"4584","Rabia ibn Abbad al-Dayli. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani; and narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi and Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf, who said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us from Ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father: Rabia ibn Abbad al-Dayli informed me, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the pre-Islamic period at the market of Dhu al-Majaz while he said: O people, say there is no god but Allah and you shall prosper. He kept repeating it time after time while people crowded upon him following him, and behind him was a squint-eyed man with two side-locks, fair-faced, saying: He is an apostate liar - twice. I asked: Who is this? They said: This is his uncle Abu Lahab."
"4585","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Mattuyah al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib from Said ibn Salama ibn Abi al-Husam from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir: He heard Rabi ibn Ibad or Abbad - Ibn Abi al-Shawarib doubted - who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) going around to people at Mina in their dwellings before he emigrated to Medina, saying: O people, Allah Almighty commands you to worship Him and associate nothing with Him. Behind him was a man saying: O people, this one commands you to abandon the religion of your fathers. I asked: Who is this man? They said: His uncle Abu Lahab."
"4586","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Wahb ibn Baqiyya from Khalid ibn Abd Allah from Muhammad ibn Amr from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from Rabia ibn Abbad al-Dayli, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Majaz following people, calling them to Allah in their dwellings, while behind him was a squint-eyed man saying: Let not this one tempt you from the religion of your fathers. I said: Who is this? They said: His uncle Abu Lahab."
"4587","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami al-Misri from Abda ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Marwazi from al-Nadr ibn Shumayl from Muhammad ibn Amr from Ibn al-Munkadir from Rabia ibn Abbad, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Majaz following people in their dwellings, calling them to Allah Almighty, while behind him was a squint-eyed man saying: O people, let not this one deceive you from your religion and the religion of your fathers. I said: Who is this? They said: His uncle Abu Lahab."
"4588","Narrated Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muhajir al-Qurashi al-Misri from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Munkadir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from his father from his grandfather: He heard Rabia ibn Abbad al-Dayali say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) going around to people at Mina in their dwellings before he emigrated to Medina, saying: O people, Allah has commanded you to worship Him and associate nothing with Him, while behind him was a squint-eyed, fair man with two side-locks saying: O people, this one commands you to abandon the religion of your fathers. I asked about him and it was said: This is his uncle Abu Lahab."
"4589","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd Allah al-Bazzaz al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi from Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith from Said ibn Salama from Zayd ibn Aslam and Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from Rabia ibn Abbad, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the market of Dhu al-Majaz before he emigrated, going around to people saying: O people, Allah commands you to worship Him and associate nothing with Him, while behind him was a man saying: O people, this one commands you to abandon the religion of your fathers. I said: Who is this? They said: His uncle Abu Lahab."
"4590","Narrated Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni from Hisham ibn Ammar from Shuayb ibn Ishaq; and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Ibn Wahb, who said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us from Said ibn Khalid al-Qarazi from Rabia ibn Abbad, who said: I saw Abu Lahab at Ukaz following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he said: O people, this one has gone astray, so let him not lead you astray from the glories of your fathers. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept away from him, while he followed him."
"4591","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Masruq ibn al-Marzuban from Ibn Abi Zaida from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Husayn ibn Abd Allah ibn Abbas, who said: I heard Rabia ibn Abbad al-Dayali say: I was with my father, a young man, watching the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) follow the tribes, with behind him a squint-eyed, fair man with a thick head of hair. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stop at a tribe and say: O sons of so-and-so, I am the Messenger of Allah to you; I command you to worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and to believe me and protect me until I convey from Allah what He sent me with. When he finished his words, the other one behind him said: O sons of so-and-so, this one wants you to cast off al-Lat and al-Uzza and your allies of the clan of Banu Malik ibn Uqaysh, for what he brought of innovation and misguidance; so do not listen and do not follow him. I said to my father: Who is this? He said: This is his uncle Abu Lahab."
"4592","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Ibn Wahb from Amr ibn al-Harith: Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him from Rabia ibn Abbad, who said: I saw Abu Lahab at Ukaz following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he said: O people, this one has gone astray, so let him not lead you astray from the glories of your fathers. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened while he was on his trail, and we followed him, as if I see him now, squint-eyed with two side-locks, the fairest and most handsome of people."
"4593","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab from Masud ibn Sad from Ata ibn al-Saib from Ibn Abbad from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing in a place before he was sent, then I saw him after he was sent standing in that spot at Arafat."
"4594","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh from his father; and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, who said: Jarir narrated to us from Ata ibn al-Saib from Ibn Rabia from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing with the polytheists at Arafat, then I saw him after he was sent standing in that very standing place, so I knew Allah had guided him to that."
"4595","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab from Masud ibn Sad from Ata ibn al-Saib from Ibn Abbad from his father, who said: A man of Banu Layth came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I adjure you - he said it three times - then on the fourth he recited to him praise of him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If any of the poets does well, you have done well."
"4596","Rabia ibn Amir ibn Bijad. Narrated Abu Husayn, al-Husayn ibn Ishaq, and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, who said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us from Ibn al-Mubarak from Yahya ibn Hassan from Rabia ibn Amir ibn Bijad, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Persist with: O Possessor of majesty and honor."
"4597","Rabia ibn al-Fadl ibn Habib al-Ansari, martyred on the day of Uhud. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Uhud, then of Banu Muawiya ibn Awf: Rabia ibn al-Fadl ibn Habib ibn Zayd ibn Tamim."
"4598","Rabia ibn al-Ghaz al-Jurashi, also said Ibn Amr, grandfather of Hisham ibn al-Ghaz. Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Lahi'a from al-Harith ibn Yazid: He heard Rabia al-Jurashi say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stay upright, and how excellent if you stay upright; guard wudu, for the best of your deeds is prayer; take care regarding the earth, for it is your mother; there is no one who does upon it good or evil but that it reports it."
"4599","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam; and narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Nadr, who said: Rayhan ibn Said narrated to us from Abbad ibn Mansur from Ayyub from Abu Qilaba from Atiyya: He heard Rabia al-Jurashi say: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was approached and it was said to him: Let your eye sleep, your ear hear, and your heart comprehend. He said: So my eye slept, my ear heard, and my heart comprehended. He said: It was said to him: A master built a house, prepared a banquet, and sent a caller; whoever answers the caller enters the house, eats of the banquet, and the master is pleased with him; whoever does not answer the caller does not enter the house, does not partake of the banquet, and the master is angry with him. The master is Allah, the caller is Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the banquet is Paradise."
"4600","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn Abi al-Sarh al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahi'a from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar from a man who narrated to him from Rabia al-Jurashi: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever conceals misappropriated spoils is like him who took them."
"4601","Rabia ibn Aktham al-Asadi, ally of Banu Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, attended Badr. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the allies of Banu Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf: Rabia ibn Aktham, of Banu Asad ibn Khuzayma."
"4602","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr of Banu Abd Shams: Rabia ibn Aktham, an ally of theirs of Banu Asad."
"4603","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Muslims martyred on the day of Uhud, then of Quraysh: Rabia ibn Aktham, ally of Banu Abd Shams, of Banu Asad."
"4604","Rabia ibn Ruwa al-Ansi. Narrated Hashim ibn Marthad al-Tabarani from Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ayyash from Isa ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz from his father from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad from his father: Rabia ibn Ruwa al-Ansi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him eating dinner, so he invited him to dinner and he ate. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? Rabia said: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. He said: Out of desire or fear? Rabia said: As for desire, by Allah it is not in your hand; as for fear, by Allah I am in lands your armies and cavalry do not reach, but I was frightened so I feared, and it was said to me: Believe, so I believed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How many an eloquent one is there among Ans. He stayed visiting the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then came to him and bade him farewell, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you sense danger, join the people of the village. So he went out, sensed danger, joined a village, and died there."
"4605","Rabia ibn Umayya ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abu Kurayb from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from his father, who said: Rabia ibn Umayya ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi was the one who shouted on the day of Arafa beneath the throat of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shout - and he was loud-voiced - O people, do you know what month this is? So he shouted, and they said: Yes, the sacred month. He said: Allah has made your blood and wealth unlawful to you until you meet your Lord, like the sanctity of this month of yours. Then he said: Shout, do you know what land this is? So he shouted; they said: Yes, the sacred land. He said: Your blood and wealth are unlawful to you until the day you meet Him, like the sanctity of this land of yours. Then he said: Shout, what day is this? So he shouted; they said: Yes, this is a sacred day, the day of the greater Hajj. He said: Allah Almighty has made your blood and wealth unlawful to you until the day you meet Him, like the sanctity of this day of yours."
"4606","Rabi al-Jarmi. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from al-Qasim ibn Abi Shayba from Salama ibn Raja from Salm ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jarmi from Sawada ibn al-Rabi, who said: My father and I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he ordered for us two camels, and said: Command your household to trim their nails, lest they wound with them the udders of their livestock when they milk."
"4607","Rabi ibn Iyas al-Ansari, a Badri. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, of Banu Lawdhan ibn Ghanm ibn Awf ibn al-Khazraj: Rabi ibn Iyas ibn Ghanm ibn Umayya ibn Lawdhan ibn Ghanm."
"4608","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, then of Banu Awf ibn al-Khazraj, then of Banu Balhubla: Rabi ibn Iyas."
"4609","Rabi al-Ansari. Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us; and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, who said: Jarir narrated to us from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr from Rabi al-Ansari: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited the nephew of Jabr al-Ansari, and his family began weeping over him. Jabr said to them: Do not harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them to weep while he is alive; when the soul is taken, let them be silent. One of them said: We did not think your death would be on your bed until you were killed in the path of Allah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is martyrdom only in being killed in the path of Allah? Then the martyrs of my umma would be few. Stabbing and plague are martyrdom, abdominal disease is martyrdom, a woman dying in childbirth is martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, collapsing is martyrdom, and pleurisy is martyrdom."
"4610","Rabi ibn Zayd, without lineage. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail from Zuhayr ibn Muawiya from Dawud ibn Abd Allah al-Awdi: He heard Wabara Abu Kurz narrate that he heard Rabi ibn Ziyad say: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was traveling, he spotted a young man of Quraysh traveling apart, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is that not so-and-so? They said: Yes. He said: Call him. He came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Why have you kept apart from the road? He said: I disliked the dust. He said: Do not keep apart from it, for by the One in whose hand is my soul, it is the fine dust of Paradise."
"4611","Ribi ibn Amr al-Ansari. Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Dirar ibn Surad from Ali ibn Hashim from Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi from his father, regarding the naming of those who were with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Ribi ibn Amr, a Badri."
"4612","Ribi ibn Rafi al-Ansari, a Badri. Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Dirar ibn Surad from Ali ibn Hashim from Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi from his father, regarding the naming of those who were with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): Ribi ibn Rafi, of Banu Amr ibn Awf, a Badri."
"4613","Ribi ibn Abi Ribi al-Ansari, a Badri. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those who attended Badr among the Ansar, of Banu al-Ajlan: Ribi ibn Abi Ribi."
"4614","Rukana ibn Abd Yazid ibn Hashim ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murra ibn Kab ibn Luayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr; his mother was the daughter of al-Ajlan, of Banu Sad ibn Layth ibn Bakr ibn Kinana; it is said he lived until the time of Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with them). Narrated Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi; and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sulayman ibn Harb; and narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal; and narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani; and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Shayban ibn Farrukh, who said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us from al-Zubayr ibn Said from Abd Allah ibn Ali ibn Yazid ibn Rukana - then I came to him in a village and he narrated to me from his father from his grandfather: He divorced his wife irrevocably, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said to him: What did you intend? He said: One. He said: By Allah? He said: By Allah. He said: It is as you intended."
"4615","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani from Ibn al-Mubarak from al-Zubayr ibn Said from Abd Allah ibn Ali ibn al-Saib from his grandfather Rukana ibn Abd Yazid: He divorced his wife irrevocably, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned it to him. He said to him: What did you mean by it? He said: One. He said: By Allah, did you mean by it only one? He said: By Allah, I meant by it only one. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: It is one."
"4616","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, who said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Rabia al-Kilabi, who said: I met a man in Mecca of the people of Asqalan called Abu al-Hasan, who narrated to me from Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali from Ibn Rukana from his father: Rukana wrestled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threw him. Rukana said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The difference between us and the polytheists is turbans over caps."
"4617","Rakb al-Misri. Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri from Yusuf ibn Adi from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Anbasa ibn Said ibn Ghunaym al-Kalai from Nasih from Rakb al-Misri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glad tidings to one who is humble without debasement, lowly in himself without misery, spends wealth gathered without disobedience, shows mercy to the poor and needy, and mixes with the people of understanding and wisdom. Glad tidings to one who is lowly in himself, whose earnings are good, whose inward is sound, and who keeps his evil from people. Glad tidings to one who acts upon his knowledge, spends the surplus of his wealth, and withholds the surplus of his speech."
"4618","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Hisham ibn Ammar from Ismail ibn Ayyash from al-Mutim ibn al-Miqdam from Nasih al-Ansi from Rakb al-Misri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glad tidings to one who is humble without debasement, lowly in himself without misery, spends wealth gathered without disobedience, shows mercy to the lowly and needy, and mixes with the people of understanding and wisdom. Glad tidings to one whose earnings are good, whose inward is sound, whose outward is noble, and who keeps his evil from people. Glad tidings to one who acts upon knowledge, spends the surplus of his wealth, and withholds the surplus of his speech."
"4619","Rabah ibn al-Rabi al-Usaydi, brother of Hanzala the scribe. Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father: Al-Muraqqi ibn Sayfi informed me - Ibn Abi Maryam said: I think it is from his father - that his grandfather Rabah ibn Rabi, brother of Hanzala the scribe, informed him: He went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign he fought, over whose vanguard was Khalid ibn al-Walid; Rabah and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a slain woman struck down by the vanguard, and they stood over her looking at her and marveling at her creation, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with them on a she-camel of his, and they parted from the woman. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She was not one to fight. Then he looked into the faces of the people and said to one of them: Catch up with Khalid ibn al-Walid: let him not kill offspring nor a hireling."
"4620","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam from Ibn Wahb from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father: Al-Muraqqi ibn Sayfi narrated to me that his grandfather Rabah ibn Rabi, brother of Hanzala the scribe, informed him: He went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like of it."
"4621","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Said ibn Abi Maryam from al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu al-Zinad: He narrated to him from al-Muraqqi ibn Sayfi from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"4622","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Said ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Karabisi from al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu al-Zinad from Muraqqi ibn Sayfi from his grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, with Khalid ibn al-Walid over the vanguard of the people; there was a slain woman on the road marvelled at for her creation, struck down by the vanguard. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and stood over her, then said to a man: Catch up with Khalid ibn al-Walid and say: let him not kill offspring nor a hireling."
"4623","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, who said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us from Umar ibn al-Muraqqi ibn Rabah from his father from his grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, when people gathered over a slain woman. He said: She was not one to fight. He said: Who is over the vanguard? They said: Khalid ibn al-Walid. So he sent a man to him saying: Command Khalid not to kill offspring nor a hireling."
"4624","Narrated Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar from Abu Kamil al-Jahdari from al-Fadl ibn Sulayman from Musa ibn Uqba: Al-Muraqqa narrated to me that he bore witness regarding his grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi al-Hanzali: He informed him that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, with Khalid ibn al-Walid as the vanguard; Rabah and the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a slain woman killed by the vanguard, gathered over her looking at her and her creation and marveling at her, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and they parted; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at her and said: She was not one to fight. Then he looked into the faces of the people and said to one of them: Catch up with Khalid ibn al-Walid and say: do not kill offspring nor a hireling."
"4625","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji, who said: Sufyan ibn Waki narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Idris from Umar ibn Muraqqa from Qays ibn Zuhayr from Rabah ibn Rabi ibn Muraqqa ibn Sayfi, who said: We fought with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he had given every three of us a camel ridden by two with one driving in the deserts, and we led in the mountains. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was walking and said to me: I see you walking, O Rabah. I said: I only just dismounted, and these two companions of mine have mounted. He went on, passed by my two companions, and they made their camel kneel and dismounted from it; when I reached them they said: Ride the front of this camel and keep upon it until we return, you and I taking turns with my companion. I said: Why? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You two have a righteous companion, so treat him well."
"4626","Rabah al-Lakhmi, grandfather of Musa ibn Ulayy. Narrated Yahya ibn Abd Allah Abu Zakariya al-Dinawari al-Basri from Said ibn Muhammad ibn Bawwab al-Husri from Mutahhar ibn al-Haytham al-Tai from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah from his father from his grandfather: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What has been born to you? He said: O Messenger of Allah, what might be born to me? Either a boy or a girl. He said: Whom will he resemble? He said: Whom might he resemble? Either his mother or his father. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Hush, do not say so; when the drop settles in the womb, Allah Almighty brings to it every lineage between it and Adam. Have you not read this verse in the Book of Allah Almighty: In whatever form He willed, He assembled you."
"4627","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd Allah Abu Zakariya al-Dinawari al-Basri from Said ibn Muhammad ibn Bawwab al-Husri from Mutahhar ibn al-Haytham al-Tai from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah from his father from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Egypt will be opened, so seek its good but do not take it as a home; the shortest-lived of people will be driven to it."
"4628","Rabah, freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr and Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, who said: Waki narrated to us from Ikrima ibn Ammar from Iyas ibn Salama from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a servant named Rabah."
"4629","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Sufyan ibn Waki from his father from Nafi ibn Umar from Ibn Abi Mulayka from Ibn Umar: Bilal informed me, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a servant named Rabah."
"4630","Rabah al-Ansari, freedman of Banu Jahjaba, martyred on the day of Yamama. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar killed on the day of Yamama: Rabah, freedman of Banu Jahjaba."
"4631","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Yamama, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Amr ibn Awf: Rabah, freedman of Banu Jahjaba."
"4632","Razin ibn Anas al-Sulami. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Rabia Fahd ibn Awf from Nail ibn Mutarrif al-Sulami from his father from his grandfather Razin ibn Anas, who said: When Islam appeared, we had a well at al-Danina, and we feared those around us would overpower us for it. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he wrote for me a letter: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); as for what follows, they shall have their well, if he is truthful. Whenever we disputed concerning it before any of the judges of Medina, they judged it for us. He said: And in the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was such-and-such, and he claimed such was the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4633","Ruqad ibn Rabia al-Uqayli. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Kathir al-Bajali from Yala ibn al-Ashdaq, who said: I met a number of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); among the truthful of them Ruqad ibn Rabia said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from us of sheep one ewe per hundred; if more, then two ewes."
"4634","Rushayd ibn Malik, Abu Umayra al-Sadi. Narrated Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Abu Nuaym; and narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Khallad ibn Yahya; and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from al-Hakam ibn Marwan; and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Yunus; and narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Amr ibn Marzuq, who said: Muarrrif ibn Wasil al-Sadi narrated to us, who said: Hafsa bint Talq narrated to me, who said: Abu Umayra Rushayd ibn Malik narrated to us, who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came to him with a tray of dates and he said to him: What is this, a gift or charity? The man said: Charity. He said: Then offer it to the people. Hasan was before him crawling in the dust; the boy took a date and put it in his mouth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) noticed him, put his finger in the boy's mouth, extracted the date, thrust it away, and said: We, the family of Muhammad, do not eat charity."
"4635","Riyab al-Muzani. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath from al-Furat ibn Abi al-Furat, who said: I heard al-Fadl ibn Talha narrate from Muawiya ibn Qurra ibn Riyab from his father: He was with his grandfather when he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as a boy, and found him with his buttons undone."
"4636","Rasim al-Abdi. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Ubayd ibn Ghannam, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Yahya ibn al-Harith from Yahya ibn Ghassan from Ibn al-Rasim from his father - of the people of Hajar, a jurist - that he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a delegation with charity, carried it to him, and he forbade them nabidh in these vessels. They returned to their land - the land of Tihama, hot - and found it unwholesome, so they returned to him the next year with their charities and said: O Messenger of Allah, you forbade us these vessels, so we left them and that was hard upon us. He said: Go and drink whatever you wish, but do not drink what its waterskin was tied upon sin."
"4637","Riya al-Juhani then al-Suhaymi. Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani; and narrated Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Tasnimi from al-Ansari from Israil ibn Yunus from Abu Ishaq from Amir al-Shabi from Riya al-Suhaymi: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote him a letter on red leather, and he patched his bucket with it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment that left him neither grazing nor resting livestock, nor family nor wealth, but took them. He escaped naked upon a mare, came to his daughter who was married among Banu Hilal - the gathering of the people was at her door-yard - and entered to her from behind the houses. She said: What is wrong with you? He said: Every evil has befallen your father; left to him neither grazing nor resting livestock, nor family nor wealth, but taken. I said: You were called to Islam. And she had embraced Islam. He said: So she cast a garment over him and he went out and said: Where is your husband? She said: In the baggage. He came to him wearing a garment such that if his head was covered with it his posteriors were exposed, and if his posteriors were covered his head was exposed. He said: What is this I see upon you? He said: Every evil has befallen me. He repeated the speech to him like what he said to his daughter. He said: And I want Muhammad before he divides my wealth. He said: Take my riding camel. He said: I have no need of it, but give me the young camel. So he took it and went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), came to him at the Fajr prayer while he was praying; when he finished his prayer he said: Stretch out your hands that I may pledge to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched out his hand, and when he wanted to strike upon it, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withheld it; he did that several times. Then he turned to him and said: Who are you? He said: Riya al-Suhaymi. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him by the upper arm and raised him from the ground, then said: Here is Riya al-Suhaymi; I wrote him a letter and he patched his bucket with it. And Riya said: My wealth and my child. He said: As for your wealth, far from it, it has been divided; as for your child and family, whomever you find of them. So he went and returned, and there his son had recognized him; he returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: This is my son. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bilal, go out with him; if he claims he is his son, hand him to him. He went out with him and said: This is my son. So he handed him to him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: He claimed he is his son, and I saw neither of them weep toward his companion. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the harshness of the bedouins."
"4638","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, who said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us from Yahya ibn Adam from Hammad ibn Salama from al-Hajjaj from Abu Ishaq from Riya al-Juhani: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote him a letter and he patched his bucket with it; a detachment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him and drove off camels of his, so he embraced Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Whatever of your wealth you find intact before it is divided, you have most right to it."
"4639","Ruqaym ibn Thabit, Abu Thabit al-Ansari, martyred on the day of Taif. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Taif, then of Banu Amr ibn Awf, then of Banu Muawiya ibn al-Harith: Ruqaym ibn Thabit ibn Thalaba."
"4640","Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar martyred on the day of Taif, then of al-Aws: Ruqaym ibn Thabit ibn Thalaba ibn Zayd ibn Lawdhan ibn Muawiya."
"4641","Rukhayla ibn Thalaba ibn Khalda al-Ansari, a Badri. 4639 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then of Banu Bayada: Rukhayla ibn Thalaba ibn Khalda."
"4642","Rawh ibn Zinba al-Judhami. 4640 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Yahya ibn Main from Hajjaj ibn Muhammad from Abu Mashar who said: When Muawiya ibn Yazid died, all the people of Sham pledged allegiance to Ibn al-Zubayr except the people of Jordan, then when the chiefs of Banu Umayya and some nobles of Sham saw that, among them Rawh ibn Zinba al-Judhami, some said to others: Sovereignty was among us, the people of Sham, and it is being transferred to Hijaz! We do not accept that."
"4643","Chapter of Za, those named Zayd. 477 - Zayd ibn al-Khattab ibn Nufayl ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Rabah ibn Abdullah ibn Qurt ibn Razah ibn Adi ibn Kab, a Badri, martyred on the day of Yamama. 4641 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those of Quraysh who attended Badr, of Banu Adi ibn Kab: Zayd ibn al-Khattab."
"4644","4642/1 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those of Quraysh who attended Badr, then of Banu Adi ibn Kab: Zayd ibn al-Khattab."
"4645","4642/2 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those of Quraysh martyred on the day of Yamama, then of Banu Adi ibn Kab: Zayd ibn al-Khattab."
"4646","4643 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami who said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad say: Zayd ibn al-Khattab was older than Umar, his kunya was Abu Abd al-Rahman, and it is said Abu Thawr."
"4647","4644 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Kill snakes, and kill the one with two stripes and the short-tailed, for they cause miscarriage and efface sight. Ibn Umar said: Abu Lubaba or Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw me chasing a snake to kill it and forbade me, so I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered killing them. He said: He later forbade killing house snakes. Al-Zuhri said: They are the awamir."
"4648","4645 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Hatim ibn Ismail from Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi from Ibn Shihab from Salim from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill snakes. Ibn Umar said: Abu Lubaba and Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw me killing a house snake, and we used to call them jinnan, and they said: O Abdullah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us from killing house snakes."
"4649","4646 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar from Bishr ibn Adam from Abu Ali al-Hanafi from Zamaa ibn Salih from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar from Abu Lubaba ibn Abd al-Mundhir and Zayd ibn al-Khattab that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing jinnan."
"4650","4647 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Abdullah ibn Sad from his uncle from his father from Salih ibn Kaysan from Ibn Shihab that Salim informed him that Abdullah ibn Umar informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Kill snakes, the one with two stripes and the short-tailed, for they seek sight and cause miscarriage. Abdullah ibn Umar said: I would not leave any snake but I killed it, until Abu Lubaba ibn al-Mundhir and Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw me pursuing a house snake and forbade me from killing it, so I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered killing them, and they said: He forbade killing house snakes."
"4651","4648 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Khallad al-Dawraqi from Muhammad ibn Hizam al-Dubai al-Basri from Ismail ibn Muhammad Abu Amir al-Ansari from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim from Abu Janab al-Kalbi from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab from his father who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Mecca toward the graves, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat by a grave, and we saw him as if conversing, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood wiping tears from his eyes, and Umar, may Allah have mercy on him, met him, being the first of us, and said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, what makes you weep? He said: I asked permission from my Lord, mighty and majestic, to visit the grave of my mother, and she was a mother with a right upon me that I seek forgiveness for her, but He forbade me. Then he gestured to us to sit, so we sat, and he said: I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so whoever of you wishes to visit, let him visit. I had forbidden you from sacrificial meat beyond three days, so eat and store as you wish. I had forbidden you vessels and commanded you vessels, so make nabidh, for vessels neither permit anything nor forbid it, and avoid every intoxicant."
"4652","478 - Zayd ibn Haritha ibn Sharahil ibn Kab ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Yazid ibn Imri al-Qays al-Kalbi, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), martyred on the day of Muta in year eight. 4649 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those who attended Badr: Zayd ibn Haritha ibn Sharahil ibn Kab ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Yazid ibn Imri al-Qays al-Kalbi, upon whom Allah and His Messenger bestowed favor."
"4653","4650 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who attended Badr of Quraysh, then of Banu Hashim: Zayd ibn Haritha."
"4654","4651 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abdullah al-Bakkai from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Zayd ibn Haritha ibn Sharahil ibn Kab ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Imri al-Qays ibn Amir ibn al-Numan ibn Amir ibn Abd Wudd ibn Awf ibn Kinana ibn Bakr ibn Awf ibn Udhra ibn Zayd Allah ibn Rufayda ibn Thawr ibn Kalb ibn Wabara ibn al-Harith ibn Qudaa, and it is said: The mother of Zayd was Suad bint Zayd of Tayy. Ibn Hisham said: Hakim ibn Hizam had come from Sham with Zayd ibn Haritha as a servant, and his aunt Khadija, then wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), asked him for him, so he gave him to her, and she gave him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who freed him and adopted him, before revelation came to him. His father came to him while he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you wish, stay with me, and if you wish, go with your father. He said: Rather I stay with you! He remained with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Allah sent him, then he believed in him, accepted Islam, and prayed with him, then when Allah revealed: Call them by their fathers, he said: I am Zayd ibn Haritha."
"4655","4652 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abdullah from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Zayd ibn Haritha accepted Islam after Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and was the first to accept Islam after him."
"4656","4653 - Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Lahia from Ibn Shihab who said: The first to accept Islam was Zayd ibn Haritha."
"4657","4654 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those killed on the day of Muta: Zayd ibn Haritha."
"4658","4655 - Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition to Muta in Jumada al-Ula of year eight, and appointed Zayd ibn Haritha, and said to them: If Zayd is struck, then Jafar ibn Abi Talib is over the people, and if Jafar is struck, then Abdullah ibn Rawaha is over the people."
"4659","4656 - Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Then the people proceeded until at the borders of Balqa they met the hosts of Heraclius, and the Muslims withdrew to a village called Muta, where the people met and the Muslims arrayed for them, placing on their right a man of Banu Udhra called Qutba ibn Qatada, and on their left a man of the Ansar called Abaya ibn Malik. Then the people met, and Zayd ibn Haritha fought with the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he died amid the spears of the enemy."
"4660","What Zayd narrated. 4657 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Kamil ibn Talha al-Jahdari, and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, both saying: Narrated Ibn Lahia from Uqayl ibn Khalid from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Usama ibn Zayd from his father that Jibril, peace be upon him, descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the first of what was revealed to him and taught him ablution and prayer, then when he finished, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took water in his hand and sprinkled his private part with it."
"4661","4658 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Ubayd ibn Yaish from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from his father from al-Bara ibn Azib from Zayd ibn Haritha who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, you made brotherhood between me and Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib."
"4662","4659 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Kurayb from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from his father from al-Bara that Zayd ibn Haritha said: O Messenger of Allah, you made brotherhood between me and Hamza."
"4663","4660 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Abdullah ibn Numayr from Hajjaj ibn Artah from al-Hakam from Miqsam from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Hamza, uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was the brother of Zayd ibn Haritha; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between them."
"4664","4661 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Salama from Ziyad ibn Abdullah al-Bakkai from Hajjaj ibn Artah from al-Hakam from Miqsam from Ibn Abbas who said: Zayd ibn Haritha said concerning the daughter of Hamza: The daughter of my brother; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between me and her father."
"4665","4662 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Ibn Lahia from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman from Urwa from Usama ibn Zayd from his father Zayd ibn Haritha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give glad tidings to those who walk in darkness to the mosques of a radiant light on the Day of Resurrection."
"4666","4663 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Abu Usama Hammad ibn Usama from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib from Usama ibn Zayd from his father Zayd ibn Haritha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with me riding behind him to an idol-stone of the idols, and we slaughtered a sheep for him then prepared it in the cooking pit, and when it cooked we took it out in our travel food, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted his she-camel with me behind him, and when we were above Mecca, Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl met him, and each greeted the other with the greeting of Jahiliyya, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Why do I see your people have come to dislike and hate you? He said: By Allah, that is from them without any vengeance from me against them, except that I see them in misguidance, so I went out seeking this religion, until I came to the rabbis of Khaybar and found them worshipping Allah yet associating with Him, so I said: By Allah, this is not the religion I seek. Then I went out until I came to the rabbis of Sham and found them worshipping Allah yet associating with Him, so I said: By Allah, this is not the religion I went out seeking. Then a rabbi of the rabbis of Sham said: You ask about a religion that we know none worships Allah with except a person in Jazira! So I went out until I came to him and told him what I went out for, and he said to me: All those you saw are in misguidance, and you ask about the religion of Allah and His angels, and a prophet has emerged in your land or is about to emerge, so return and believe in him and have faith in him! So I returned and have not yet met any prophet. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his she-camel kneel and placed the travel food before him, and said: What is this? He said: A sheep we slaughtered for such-and-such idol-stone. Zayd ibn Amr said: We do not eat anything slaughtered for other than Allah! Then we parted. He said: And Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl died before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone."
"4667","4664 - Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Wahb ibn Baqiyya from Khalid from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib from Usama ibn Zayd from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with me riding behind him. Then he mentioned similar."
"4668","4665 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Abu Usama from Muhammad ibn Amr from Abu Salama and Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib from Usama ibn Zayd from his father who said: I circumambulated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day and I touched some of the idols, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do not touch them. So I said: I shall repeat until I see what he says! Then I touched one, and he said: Were you not forbidden this? He said: By the One who honored him and sent down the Book upon him, he never touched any idol after that until Allah honored him and sent down the Book upon him."
"4669","4666 - Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Ibrahim ibn Isa al-Tanukhi from Ziyad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furat al-Qazzaz from his father from his grandfather from Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathila from Zayd ibn Haritha who said: I was a boy at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Come with us to a man whose affair we have seen. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked with his companions until they entered between two walls in a long lane, reaching a small door at the end of the lane, and entered a house, seeing no one in the house but one woman, and there was a huge waterskin full of water. They said: We see a waterskin but see no carrier; speak to the woman! She pointed to a velvet cloth in the corner and said: Look under the velvet. They uncovered it, and beneath it was a man. He raised his head, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May the face be deformed. He said: O Muhammad, why do you revile me? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I have hidden something for you, tell me what it is? And he said to his companions: I have hidden the chapter of Dukhan for him! He said: Dukh! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Be gone, what Allah wills happens. Then he left."
"4670","4667 - Narrated Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi from Muhammad ibn Mujib Abu Hammam al-Dallal from Ibrahim ibn Tahman from Jabir from al-Shaabi from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil from Zayd ibn Haritha who said: I gave a horse of mine in charity, then I saw its daughter being stood in the market, and I wanted to buy it, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about it."
"4671","4668 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Layth al-Jawhari from Abu Hammam al-Walid ibn Shuja from his father from Ziyad ibn Khaythama from Dawud ibn Abi Hind from Abu al-Aliya from Zayd ibn Haritha that he carried a horse in the path of Allah, then he later found it sold in the market, emaciated and thin, so he bargained for it, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: I had carried a horse in the path of Allah, and I found it sold in the market for a small price, emaciated and thin, and I recognized its pedigree, so shall I buy it? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him from buying it."
"4672","4669 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj from Kathir ibn Abi Kathir from Ali ibn Abdullah from Zayd ibn Haritha that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the time of the dawn prayer. He said: Pray it with us today and tomorrow. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the valley of Namira of Juhfa, he prayed it when the first dawn rose, then when he was at Dhu Tuwa he delayed it until the people said: Has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been taken that we should pray? Then he prayed before the sun, turned to the people and said: What did you say? They said: If only we had prayed! He said: Had you done so, punishment would have befallen you. Then he called the questioner and said: Its time is between my two prayers."
"4673","479 - Zayd ibn Bawla, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4670 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Musa ibn Ismail from Hafs ibn Umar al-Shanni from Umar ibn Murra who said: I heard Bilal ibn Yasar ibn Zayd, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: I heard my father narrate from my grandfather that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: I seek forgiveness from Allah besides whom there is no god, and I repent to Him, he is forgiven, even if he fled from battle."
"4674","480 - Zayd ibn Sahl, Abu Sahl, Abu Talha al-Ansari, Aqabi, Badri, naqib. 4671 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of the people of Aqaba of the Ansar, then of Banu Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar, who are Banu Jadila: Abu Talha Sahl ibn Zayd ibn al-Aswad, and he is a naqib. Thus Ibn Lahia said Sahl ibn Zayd in the naming of those who attended Aqaba, and he said it correctly in the naming of those who attended Badr."
"4675","4672 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who attended Aqaba of the Ansar: Abu Talha Zayd ibn Sahl of Banu al-Najjar, and he attended Badr."
"4676","4673 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those who attended Aqaba of the Ansar, then of Banu Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar ibn Aws: Abu Talha, whose name is Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manat ibn Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik."
"4677","4674 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqa from al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Sudai from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Said from his father from Ibn Ishaq who said: Abu Talha Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manat ibn Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar."
"4678","4675 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Abu Talha Zayd ibn Sahl; I heard Ibn Idris say: One of his children told me."
"4679","4676 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Jafar ibn Sulayman from Thabit from Anas who said: Abu Talha proposed to Umm Sulaym before he accepted Islam, and she said: I am indeed desirous of you, and none like you is rejected, but you are a disbelieving man and I am a Muslim woman, so if you accept Islam, that is my dowry and I ask nothing else! So Abu Talha accepted Islam and married her."
"4680","4677 - Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Qutayba ibn Said from Muhammad ibn Musa al-Fitri from Abdullah ibn Abi Talha who said: Abu Talha married Umm Sulaym, and the dowry between them was Islam. Umm Sulaym accepted Islam before Abu Talha, then he proposed to her, and she said: I have accepted Islam, so if you accept Islam I shall marry you! So he accepted Islam, and the dowry between them was Islam."
"4681","4678 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith from his father from Harb ibn Maymun from al-Nadr ibn Anas from Anas that Umm Sulaym married Abu Talha for his Islam."
"4682","4679 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Salih ibn Abdullah al-Tirmidhi from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Abbad ibn Mansur from Anas who said: Abu Talha al-Ansari took a mosque in his house and sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with me and Abu Talha, with Umm Sulaym behind us."
"4683","4680 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Ali ibn al-Jad from Shuba from Thabit from Anas who said: Abu Talha would not fast at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) because of campaigns, then when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, I never saw him break fast except on the day of Adha or Fitr."
"4684","4681 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit from Anas that Abu Talha fasted continuously after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he died."
"4685","4682 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Hudba ibn Khalid from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit from Anas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between Abu Ubayda and Abu Talha."
"4686","Mention of the death of Abu Talha, about which there is disagreement; it is said he died campaigning at sea, and said he died in Medina. As for those who said he died campaigning at sea: 4683 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan from his father from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit and Ali ibn Zayd from Anas ibn Malik who said: Abu Talha went out campaigning at sea and died in the ship, and they found no place to bury him, so they waited with him six days until after seven they found a place to bury him, and he had not changed as he was. As for those who said he died in Medina."
"4687","4684 - Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr who said: Abu Talha died in year thirty-four, and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed over him; aged seventy, and his name is Zayd ibn Sahl."
"4688","4685 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, both saying: Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr who said: Abu Talha Zayd ibn Sahl died in year thirty-four, and Uthman ibn Affan prayed over him; he died at seventy years, and it is also said Abu Talha died in year thirty-two."
"4689","What Abu Talha narrated; Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha. 4686 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba that he heard Ibn Abbas say: I heard Abu Talha say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image of statues."
"4690","4687 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa from al-Haytham ibn Marwan from Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Sumay from Rawh ibn al-Qasim from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4691","4688 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu Asim (h). And narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Bablutti, both from Ibn Abi Dhib from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4692","4689 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from al-Qanabi (h). And narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba (h). And narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi - who said: Narrated Sufyan from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4693","4690 - Narrated Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Yunus ibn Yazid from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah that he heard Ibn Abbas say: I heard Abu Talha say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4694","4691 - Narrated Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri from his father from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad from Ibn Akhi al-Zuhri from his uncle from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba that he heard Ibn Abbas say: I heard Abu Talha say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4695","4692/1 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar from Abu Maryam from al-Walid ibn Kathir from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba that Abdullah ibn al-Abbas narrated to him that Abu Talha said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4696","4692/2 - Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Hawrani from al-Walid ibn Muslim from al-Awzai from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor an image."
"4697","4693 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Abu Muawiya from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah from al-Hasan ibn Sad from Ibn Abbas from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined between Hajj and Umra."
"4698","4694 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Khalid al-Ahmar and Abu Muawiya (h). And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaida and Abu Muawiya, from Hajjaj from al-Hasan ibn Sad from Ibn Abbas who said: Abu Talha narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined between Hajj and Umra."
"4699","Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani from Abu Talha. 4695 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad (h). And narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Said ibn Sulayman - both saying: Narrated Khalid ibn Abdullah from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih from Said ibn Yasar from Zayd ibn Khalid from Abu Talha al-Ansari who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor a statue."
"4700","4696 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Qutayba ibn Said from Layth ibn Sad from Bukayr ibn Abdullah from Bishr ibn Said from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani from Abu Talha who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is an image."
"4701","4697 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Jarir from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih from Said ibn Yasar from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani from Abu Talha al-Ansari who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Angels do not enter a house in which there is a dog nor a statue."
"4702","4698 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri from Ahmad ibn Salih from Ibn Wahb from Amr ibn al-Harith that Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him that Bishr ibn Said narrated to him that Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani narrated to him that Abu Talha narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is an image."
"4703","Anas ibn Malik from Abu Talha. 4699 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Amr ibn Marzuq from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I was among those upon whom drowsiness was poured on the day of Uhud."
"4704","4700 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha that he was among those overcome by drowsiness that day, saying: The sword would fall from his hand and he would take it, and fall from my hand then I would take it, from drowsiness."
"4705","4701 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam from Abd al-Ala from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered some thirty-odd chiefs of Quraysh, and they were thrown into a dry well of the wells of Badr where they decayed, and when he prevailed over a people he would stay in the courtyard three days. On the third day he ordered his mount and its saddle was fastened, then he walked with his companions following him, saying: We do not see him going except for some need! Then he came to the edge of the well and said: O so-and-so son of so-and-so and O so-and-so son of so-and-so, would that you had obeyed Allah and His Messenger, have you found what your Lord promised true, for we have found what our Lord promised us true. Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, do you speak to bodies with no souls in them? He said: By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, you are not more hearing of what I say than they. Said said: Qatada said: Allah gave them life for him until they heard his word."
"4706","4702 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Muadh ibn Muadh and Abd al-Ala (h). And narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Qutayba ibn Said from Muadh ibn Muadh - both saying: Narrated Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) overcame a people he liked to stay in their courtyard three days or three nights."
"4707","4703 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Abu al-Jumahir from Said ibn Bashir from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Khaybar in the morning he recited this verse: When we descend in the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those warned."
"4708","4704 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi from Umayya ibn Bistam from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Khaybar in the morning while they had taken their spades and baskets and gone out to their fields, and when they saw the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the army they turned back fleeing, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Khaybar is ruined; when we descend in the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those warned."
"4709","4705 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Sulayman ibn al-Mughira al-Ansi from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so that my knee touched his knee, and he was silent about them, until at dawn when the milker went to his udder and the farmer to his field, he raided them and said: When we descend in the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those warned."
"4710","4706 - Narrated Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr and Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Missisi, both saying: Narrated Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Dimashqi from Said ibn Bashir from Qatada from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in his talbiya: Labbayk with Hajj and Umra together."
"4711","4707 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Abu Bakr ibn Khallad al-Bahili from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I watched the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions on the day of Uhud, and saw no one but nodding under his shield from drowsiness."
"4712","4708 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Abu Bakr ibn Khallad from Muhammad ibn Abi Adi from Humayd from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I was among those upon whom drowsiness was poured on the day of Uhud."
"4713","4709 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah reward you, O company of Ansar, with good, for you, as far as I know, are chaste and patient."
"4714","4710 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi and Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji, both saying: Narrated Abda ibn Abdullah al-Saffar from Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith from Muhammad ibn Thabit al-Bunani from his father from Anas from Abu Talha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Convey peace to your people, for they, as far as I know, are chaste and patient."
"4715","4711 - Narrated Zakariya ibn Hamdawayh al-Saffar from Affan ibn Muslim (h). And narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi, both saying: Narrated Hammam who said: It was said to Matar while I was with him: From whom did al-Hasan take ablution from what fire touched? He said: He took it from Anas, and Anas took it from Abu Talha, and Abu Talha took it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4716","4712 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Qadi from Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi from Qays ibn al-Rabi from Ismail al-Suddi from Yahya ibn Abbad from Anas from Abu Talha that he bought wine for orphans, then when wine was forbidden he said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I make it vinegar? He said: No."
"4717","4713 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abd al-Samad ibn Hayyan from Sufyan al-Thawri from al-Suddi from Abu Hubayra Yahya ibn Abbad from Anas from Abu Talha from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar."
"4718","4714 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Mutamir from Layth from Yahya ibn Abbad from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I bought wine for orphans in my care! He said: Pour out the wine and break the jars. I said: O Messenger of Allah, it belongs to orphans! He said: Pour out the wine and break the jars."
"4719","4715 - Narrated Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib from Umar ibn Rudayh from Ata ibn Abi Maymuna from Anas from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mixed drinks."
"4720","4716 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn al-Harish al-Ahwazi from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib from Umar ibn Rudayh from Ata ibn Abi Maymuna from Anas from Abu Talha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade joining drinks."
"4721","4717 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from my brother Sulayman ibn Bilal from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him."
"4722","4718 - Narrated Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Mukharrimi and Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki, both saying: Narrated Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Jasr ibn Farqad from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw him cheerful and bright-faced, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I never saw you more cheerful than today. He said: What prevents me, when the angel told me that whoever sends blessing upon you, I and my angels send ten upon him, and whoever greets you, I and my angels greet him ten times."
"4723","4719 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Baghawi from Salih ibn Malik al-Khuwarizmi from Salih al-Murri from Thabit from Anas from Abu Talha who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw in his cheer and openness what I never saw before, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I never saw you in such a state. He said: What prevents me, O Abu Talha, when Jibril, peace be upon him, just left me bringing glad tidings from my Lord, saying: Allah sent me to you to give you glad tidings that none of your nation sends one blessing upon you but Allah and His angels send ten upon him for it."
"4724","4720 - Narrated Ali ibn Abdullah al-Farghani from Ibrahim ibn al-Walid al-Tabarani from his father from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salama al-Majishun from al-Zuhri from Anas ibn Malik from Abu Talha who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the lines of his face shining, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I never saw you more cheerful nor brighter than today. He said: Why should I not be cheerful when Jibril, peace be upon him, just parted from me saying: O Muhammad, whoever of your nation sends one blessing upon you, Allah writes ten good deeds for him, erases ten evil deeds from him, raises him ten degrees for it, and the angel says to him as he said to you. I said: O Jibril, what angel is that? He said: Allah, mighty and majestic, entrusted with you an angel from your creation until He raises you; none of your nation sends blessing upon you but he says: And upon you may Allah send blessing."
"4725","4721 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh from his father (h). And narrated Abd al-Warith ibn Ibrahim al-Askari from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani from Hammad ibn Amr al-Nasibi from Zayd ibn Rufay from al-Zuhri from Anas ibn Malik from Abu Talha who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his face radiant with joy, and said: O Messenger of Allah, you are in a state I never saw you in. He said: What prevents me, Jibril, peace be upon him, just came saying: Give your nation glad tidings that whoever sends one blessing upon you, ten good deeds are written for him, ten evil deeds expiated from him, ten degrees raised for him, Allah returns to him the like of his word, and it is presented to him on the Day of Resurrection."
"4726","4722 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, both saying: Narrated Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama that Aban ibn Abi Ayyash informed them from Anas ibn Malik from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon a man saying: O Allah, I ask You as all praise is Yours, there is no god but You, the Benefactor, Originator of heavens and earth, Lord of majesty and honor. He said: He has asked Allah by the name by which if He is called He answers."
"4727","4723 - Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Harun ibn Musa ibn Rashid al-Mustamli al-Kabir Mukhula from Umar ibn Ayyub al-Mawsili from Masad ibn Uqba from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq from Anas ibn Malik from Abu Talha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to the poor is charity, and to a relative is charity and kinship."
"4728","Abdullah ibn Abi Talha from his father. 4724 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal (h). And narrated Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Tayalisi and Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi, both saying: Narrated Abu al-Walid (h). And narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami - who said: Narrated Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit from Sulayman, freedman of al-Hasan ibn Ali, from Abdullah ibn Abi Talha from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came one day with cheer visible on his face, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, we see cheer on your face we never saw. He said: An angel came to me saying: Your Lord says to you: Does it not please you that none of your nation sends blessing upon you but I send ten upon him? And none greets you but I greet him ten times? I said: Yes."
"4729","4725 - Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim al-Jawhari and Zakariya ibn Hamdawayh al-Saffar, both saying: Narrated Affan ibn Muslim (h). And narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad - both saying: Narrated Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad from Uthman ibn Hakim from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha from his father from his grandfather who said: We were sitting in courtyards when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed us and said: What is with you sitting on the roads? We said: O Messenger of Allah, we sat conversing remembering Allah. He said: Give gatherings their due. We said: O Messenger of Allah, what is their due? He said: Lowering the gaze, returning greeting, guiding the way, and good speech."
"4730","4726 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar and Ahmad ibn Abdullah al-Bazzar al-Tustari, both saying: Narrated Muhammad ibn Miskin al-Yamani from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Janah from Yahya ibn Shuba who said: I heard Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha narrate from his father from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and bathes, goes early and arrives early, draws near the imam and listens without idle talk on Friday, Allah writes for him for every step to the mosque fasting a year and its standing."
"4731","4727 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri from Ahmad ibn Salih from Ibn Wahb from Amr ibn al-Harith from Umara ibn Ghaziyya from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha from his father that Abu Talha invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Umayr ibn Talha when he died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them and prayed over him in their house, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in front, Abu Talha behind him, and Umm Sulaym, with none else with them."
"4732","4728 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Qadi from Nasr ibn Ali from Harami ibn Umara from Shuba from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs from al-Zuhri from Ibn Abi Talha from his father from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Perform ablution from what fire touched."
"4733","4729 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi from Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki from Hatim from Muawiya ibn Abi Muzarrid from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha from his father from Abu Talha who said: I entered the mosque and recognized hunger on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I asked Umm Sulaym: Have you anything? She gestured with her hands saying: I have something. I said: Prepare and knead, and I sent Anas saying: Go to him, whisper in his ear and invite him. When Anas approached, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This man has brought you good; for what did your father send you inviting us? Anas said: Yes. He received him saying: Stand, in the name of Allah. Anas ran back to Abu Talha saying: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coming to you with the people. Abu Talha said: I met him at the door at the stair landing saying: What have you done to us, O Messenger of Allah? I only recognized hunger on your face and prepared something for you to eat. He said: Enter and rejoice. He entered and came to their dish, leveling it with his hand, then said: Is there any condiment? He spread his hand and butter poured from their skin, then said: Admit ten by ten. They were about a hundred, entering and eating until full, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of the remainder: Eat you and your dependents. So they ate and were full."
"4734","Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari from Abu Talha. 4730 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Muadh from his father from Shuba from Amr ibn Dinar from Yahya ibn Jada from Abdullah Abd ibn al-Qari from Abu Talha from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Perform ablution from what fire touched."
"4735","Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Abu Talha. 4731 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Malik from Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubaydullah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud that he visited Abu Talha al-Ansari in illness, saying: We found with him Sahl ibn Hunayf. Abu Talha called a man and removed a mat beneath him, and Sahl ibn Hunayf said to him: Why remove it? He said: Because it has images, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about them what you know. Sahl said: Did he not say: Except what is a figure on cloth? He said: Yes, but it is better for my soul."
"4736","4732 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Jarir from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Salim Abu al-Nadr from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba who said: I went with Uthman ibn Hunayf to Abu Talha visiting him, and found beneath him a mat with pictures. When we sat he said: Remove this from us. Uthman said to him: Did you not hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid images except a figure on cloth or cloth with a figure? He said: Yes, but I dislike it, so he removed it."
"4737","4733 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Shuayb ibn Yahya from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Nadr from Abdullah al-Khawlani from Abu Talha, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he visited Sahl while ill, with beneath Sahl a mat with statues, and Sahl said: Remove this mat from beneath me. Abu Talha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about images: Angels do not enter it except what is a figure on cloth, there is no harm in it. Sahl said: Even so, remove it."
"4738","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri from Abu Talha. 4734 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Said ibn Mansur from Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman from his father from his father from his grandfather from Abu Talha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate pieces of dried cheese and performed ablution from it."
"4739","Ismail ibn Bashir ibn Maghala from Abu Talha. 4735 - Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih (h). And narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr - both saying: Narrated al-Layth from Yahya ibn Sulaym ibn Zayd, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard Ismail ibn Bashir, freedman of Banu Maghala, say: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah and Abu Talha ibn Sahl al-Ansaris say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man abandons a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated but Allah abandons him where he loves His help, and no one aids a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated but Allah aids him where he loves His help."
"4740","Ishaq ibn Abdullah from Abu Talha. 4736 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi from Humayd from Thabit from Ishaq ibn Abdullah from Abu Talha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed two white rams, saying at the first: From Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and at the second: From whoever of my nation believed in me and affirmed me."
"4741","481 - Zayd ibn Thabit al-Ansari, kunya Abu Said, and said Abu Kharija. 4737 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Zayd ibn Thabit ibn al-Dahhak."
"4742","4738 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Yahya ibn Said from Abu Jafar al-Khatmi from my uncle from my grandfather Uqba ibn Fakih who said: I went out to Zayd ibn Thabit and he came out to me wearing a waist-wrapper with a spear in his hand, so I said: O Abu Kharija, what is with the spear at this hour? He said: I was seeking this wicked beast by whose killing Allah writes a good deed and erases an evil deed, and it is the gecko."
"4743","4739 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani from Ahmad ibn Ali ibn Shawdhab al-Wasiti from Yaqub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri from al-Qasim ibn Abi al-Zinad from his brother from his father from Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit that Marwan said to Zayd ibn Thabit: O Abu Said."
"4744","4740 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Jafar ibn Humayd from Ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father from Kharija ibn Zayd who said: They said to Zayd ibn Thabit: O Abu Said."
"4745","4741 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Zayd ibn Thabit, kunya Abu Said."
"4746","4742 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i and Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf, both saying: Narrated Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father from Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit from his father who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Medina when I was eleven years old."
"4747","4743 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani from Yaqub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri from Ismail ibn Qays from his father from Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit from Zayd who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me on the day of Khandaq and clothed me a Qibti garment."
"4748","4744 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Kurayb from Yahya ibn Yala from his father from Ghaylan ibn Jami from Abu Ishaq from Musab ibn Sad who said: Uthman said: Which people write best? They said: Zayd ibn Thabit."
"4749","4745 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Abi Karima al-Harrani from Muhammad ibn Salama from Abu Abd al-Rahim from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa from Abu Ishaq from Musab ibn Sad who said: Uthman, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Call for me Zayd ibn Thabit, scribe of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4750","4746 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Razin al-Rummani from al-Shaabi that Zayd ibn Thabit said four takbirs over his mother, and I did not think it a hadd, then a mount was brought and Ibn Abbas held the stirrup for him, and Zayd said to him: Leave it. Ibn Abbas said: Thus we do with great scholars."
"4751","4747 - Narrated Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Abu Mushir from Said ibn Abd al-Aziz who said: The scholars after Muadh ibn Jabal were Abdullah ibn Masud, Abu al-Darda, Salman, and Abdullah ibn Salam, and the scholars after them were Zayd ibn Thabit, then after Zayd were Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas."
"4752","4748 - Narrated al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi from Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi from Salama ibn al-Fadl from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Muhammad ibn Jafar from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Abdullah ibn al-Arqam as scribe, writing to kings, and his trust reached that he would write to a king then be ordered to fold and seal without reading for his trustworthiness, and he also took Zayd ibn Thabit as scribe, writing revelation and to kings, and when Abdullah ibn al-Arqam and Zayd ibn Thabit were absent and he needed to write to commanders and kings or a grant, he ordered whoever was present to write, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, Uthman ibn Affan, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Zayd ibn Thabit, al-Mughira ibn Shuba, Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, and Khalid ibn Said ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them, and others of the Arabs wrote for him."
"4753","4749 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Waki from Hammad ibn Salama from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar who said: We sat with Ibn Abbas at the funeral of Zayd ibn Thabit and he said: Much knowledge was buried today."
"4754","4750 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Hammad ibn Zayd from Yahya ibn Said who said: Abu Hurayra said when Zayd ibn Thabit died: Today the scholar of this nation died, and may Allah make in Ibn Abbas a successor from him."
"4755","4751 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Mab Bahram al-Idhaji from Malik ibn Said al-Qaysi from Rawh ibn Ubada from Abu Amir al-Khazzaz from Ali ibn Zayd from Said ibn al-Musayyib who said: I witnessed the funeral of Zayd ibn Thabit, and when he was lowered into his grave Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, whoever wishes to know how knowledge departs, thus departs knowledge; by Allah, much knowledge departed today. Said ibn al-Musayyib said: And the sayer of much knowledge departed with him means Ibn Abbas."
"4756","4752 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Zayd ibn Thabit died in year forty-five, and Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit died in year ninety-nine."
"4757","4753 - Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr who said: Zayd ibn Thabit died in year forty-five, aged fifty-six, and some say: He died in year forty-eight aged fifty-nine, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him on the day of Khandaq when fifteen, and Khandaq was in Shawwal year four, and there is disagreement about his death."
"4758","4754 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father, may Allah have mercy on him, who said: It reached me that Zayd ibn Thabit died in year fifty-one."
"4759","4755 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali from Dawud ibn Rushayd from al-Haytham ibn Adi who said: Zayd ibn Thabit died in year fifty-five."
"4760","Abdullah ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit. 4756 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abdullah ibn Numayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabana."
"4761","4757 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi (h). And narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from al-Qanabi (h). And narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father (h). And narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba - who said: Narrated Sufyan from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya."
"4762","4758 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya to be sold by its estimate as dates, and permitted nothing else."
"4763","4759 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from al-Awzai from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya for dates and fresh dates, and permitted nothing else."
"4764","4760 - Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi from Yahya ibn Hamza from al-Zubaydi from al-Zuhri from Salim from his father from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by measure for its estimate of dry dates."
"4765","4761 - Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Ali ibn Mabad from Khalid ibn Hayyan from Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud from al-Zuhri from Salim from his father from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya for fresh or dry dates, and permitted nothing else."
"4766","4762 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ubaydullah ibn Sad from his uncle from his father from Salih ibn Kaysan from Ibn Shihab that Salim informed him that he heard Abdullah ibn Umar that Zayd ibn Thabit informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya for fresh and dry dates, and permitted nothing else."
"4767","4763 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Yahya ibn Said from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted araya to be sold by its estimate as dates."
"4768","4764 - Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi from Qabisa ibn Uqba from Sufyan from Yahya ibn Said and Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted araya to be sold by its estimate as dates."
"4769","4765 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Yahya ibn Said from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its estimate as dates."
"4770","4766 - Narrated Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri from his father from Anas ibn Iyad from Yahya ibn Said from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of ariya taken by a household by its estimate, eating it as fresh dates."
"4771","4767 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Malik from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the owner of ariya to sell it by its estimate by measure."
"4772","4768 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan (h). And narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Yusuf al-Qadi, both saying: Narrated Sulayman ibn Harb - both saying: Narrated Hammad ibn Zayd from Ayyub from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its estimate."
"4773","4769 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Ayyub from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya."
"4774","4770 - Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath from Hammad ibn Salama from Ayyub and Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar who said: Zayd ibn Thabit said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya, one or two palms gifted to a man, sold by its estimate as dates."
"4775","4771 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Yahya ibn Said from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its measure."
"4776","4772 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from al-Qanabi from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its estimate by measure."
"4777","4773 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi from Yaqub ibn Abi Abbad al-Makki from Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn Uqba from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya to be sold by its estimate by measure."
"4778","4774 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma from Juwayriya ibn Asma from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya to be sold by its estimate by measure."
"4779","4775 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin from Zakariya ibn Yahya, scribe of al-Umari, from al-Mufaddal ibn Faddala from Abdullah ibn Sulayman al-Tawil from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its estimate."
"4780","4776 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Nafi al-Tahhan al-Misri from Ahmad ibn Salih from Anbasa ibn Khalid from Yunus from Abu al-Zinad from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the sale of araya by its estimate."
"4781","4777 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Ahmad ibn Jamil al-Marwazi from Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak from Musa ibn Uqba from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted araya by its estimate by measure."
"4782","4778 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Mansur ibn Abi Muzahim from Ibn Abi al-Zinad from Musa ibn Uqba from Nafi from Zayd ibn Thabit - then mentioned similar."
"4783","4779 - Narrated Jafar ibn Ahmad al-Shami al-Kufi from Abu Kurayb from Khalid ibn Makhlad from Nafi from Abu Nuaym from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the owner of ariya to sell it by its estimate."
"4784","4780 - Narrated Ubaydullah ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abdullah ibn Numayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqala and muzabana."
"4785","4781 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba (h). And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba - both saying: Narrated Husayn ibn Muhammad from Jarir ibn Hazim from Abu al-Zinad from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: I bought oil in the market, and a man came to me profiting me until I was pleased, then when I took his hand to strike upon it a man behind me took my arm and held my hand, and I turned to him and it was Zayd ibn Thabit, saying: Do not sell it until you take possession to your house, for the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling that."
"4786","Narrated Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Abu al-Zinad from Ubayd ibn Hunayn from Ibn Umar, who said: I bought oil in the market, and when I had taken full possession of it a man met me and offered me a good profit for it, so I wanted to strike my hand upon his hand (to seal the sale), but a man behind me seized my forearm; I turned around and there was Zayd ibn Thabit. He said: Do not sell it where you bought it until you bring it to your lodging, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that goods be sold where they are bought until the merchants bring them to their lodgings."
"4787","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Ubayd ibn Ya'ish from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Abu al-Zinad from Ubayd from Ibn Umar, who said: Oil arrived from Sham, and I bought several camel-loads of it and finished purchasing them, then a man came to me and offered me a profit on them, so I stretched out my hand to conclude the sale with him, when a man seized my hand from behind me; I looked and there was Zayd ibn Thabit. He said: Do not sell it until you move it to your lodging, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that."
"4788","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri from Zayd ibn Thabit. Narrated Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar from Yazid ibn Harun from Sa'id al-Jurayri (H). And narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba (H). And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba - the latter two said: narrated Isma'il ibn Ulayya from Sa'id al-Jurayri from Abu Nadra from Abu Sa'id: Zayd ibn Thabit informed me, saying: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a garden belonging to Banu al-Najjar, riding his mule with us alongside him, he said: Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave; seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the Fire. We said: We seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave; we seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the Fire. He said: Seek refuge with Allah from tribulations, what is apparent of them and what is hidden. We said: We seek refuge with Allah from tribulations, what is apparent of them and what is hidden. He said: Seek refuge with Allah from the tribulation of the Dajjal. They said: We seek refuge with Allah from the tribulation of the Dajjal."
"4789","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn al-Musawir al-Jawhari from Affan ibn Muslim from Wuhayb ibn Khalid from Dawud ibn Abi Hind from Abu Nadra from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, the orators of the Ansar stood and said: O company of Muhajirun, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send a man from among you, he would pair him with a man from among us, so we consider that this matter should be handled by two men, a man from among you and a man from among us. Then Zayd ibn Thabit stood and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was from among the Muhajirun, and we were the supporters (Ansar) of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we are the supporters of whoever takes his place. Then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: May Allah reward you with good, O company of the Ansar, as a tribe, and may He keep your speaker firm; by Allah, had you said otherwise we would not have reconciled with you."
"4790","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Salih al-Shirazi from Amr ibn Hakam from Shu'ba from Amr ibn Murra from Abu al-Bukhtari from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My Companions are one party and the people are another party. Then Zayd ibn Thabit and Rafi' ibn Khadij said: He spoke the truth."
"4791","Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi from Zayd ibn Thabit. Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Uqba ibn Mukram (H). And narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani from Abu Kurayb - both said: narrated Yunus ibn Bukayr from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman from al-Zuhri from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: I heard Zayd ibn Thabit say: When revelation was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it weighed heavily on that account, and his forehead would drip with sweat like pearls even in cold weather. The wording is that of Uqba ibn Mukram."
"4792","Sahl ibn Abi Hathma from Zayd ibn Thabit. Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi from Harun ibn al-Mughira from Anbasa ibn Sa'id from Zakariyya ibn Khalid from Abu al-Zinad from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Sahl ibn Abi Hathma from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: They used to trade in fruits before they appeared (ripened), then they would dispute before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Since you do this, then do not trade until its soundness becomes evident."
"4793","Anas ibn Malik from Zayd ibn Thabit. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Yusuf al-Qadi, who said: narrated Amr ibn Marzuq from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from Anas from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked toward Yemen and said: O Allah, turn their hearts (toward us), and bless for us our sa' and our mudd."
"4794","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from Anas ibn Malik from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked toward Yemen and said: O Allah, turn their hearts. And he looked toward Iraq and said: O Allah, turn their hearts. And he looked toward Sham and said: O Allah, turn their hearts, and bless for us our sa' and our mudd."
"4795","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from his father, who said: I found in my father's book in his handwriting: narrated Mustalim ibn Sa'id from Mansur ibn Zadhan from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for his umma and said: O Allah, turn their hearts toward Your religion and surround those behind them with Your mercy. And he did not mention Zayd ibn Thabit."
"4796","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Yusuf al-Qadi and Abu Khalifa, who said: narrated Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Hisham al-Dastuwai from Qatada from Anas from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: We ate sahur with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we went out to the prayer. I said: How long was it between the adhan, the iqama, and the sahur? He said: The extent of (reciting) fifty verses."
"4797","Narrated Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi from al-Qasim ibn Isa al-Tai from Hushaym from Mansur ibn Zadhan from Qatada from Anas from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: We ate sahur with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we went out to the prayer. I said: How long was it between the adhan, the iqama? He said: The extent of (reciting) fifty verses."
"4798","With his chain (of transmission) he said: We ate sahur with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we went out and prayed."
"4799","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from Salim ibn Nuh from Umar ibn Amir from Qatada from Anas from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: We ate sahur with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we did not rise until he prayed the morning prayer. I said: And how long was that? He said: The extent of what a person recites as fifty verses."
"4800","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from al-Sari ibn Yahya from Thabit al-Bunani from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was walking with Zayd ibn Thabit, and he shortened his steps, then said: Do you know why I walked with you in this manner? I said: No. He said: So that our steps in walking to the prayer may be numerous. And al-Sari ibn Yahya did not raise it (to the Prophet)."
